(window.PHASE_DATA=window.PHASE_DATA||{})[2]={"pages": [{"pdf": 75, "printed": 48, "ch": 0, "text": "PHASE 2\n\nSchools of Thought, Credit and\nBanking\nChapters 7–13\n\nStiglitz and Weiss (1981); Tirole; Fama (1970); Stiglitz (2011); Lavoie"}, {"pdf": 76, "printed": 49, "ch": 7, "text": "CHAPTER 7\n\nStiglitz and Weiss (1981): Credit Rationing in Markets with Imperfect Information Source line Joseph E. Stiglitz and Andrew Weiss, “Credit Rationing in Markets with Imperfect Information”, American Economic Review 71(3), June 1981, pp. 393–410. Your file is the complete JSTOR copy. All page numbers below are the journal’s printed pages. The paper is 18 pages, but about half of it is proofs; this chapter gives you the argument, the economics behind each theorem, and a worked example that you can reproduce on a blackboard.\n\n7.1 The puzzle and why it is a big claim\nOrdinary supply and demand says that if more people want loans than banks are willing to supply, the price of loans (the interest rate) rises until the two match. Stiglitz and Weiss open with this very point: “Perhaps the most basic tenet of economics is that market equilibrium entails supply equalling demand” (p. 393). Yet credit rationing is observed in the real world. The two usual explanations are that it is temporary (prices are sticky for a while) or that the government forbids a higher price (usury laws). Their claim is stronger: “in equilibrium a loan market may be characterized by credit rationing” (p. 393). A lender who can raise the rate and chooses not to is not making a mistake. It is maximising profit. In plain words\n\nA bank is not selling a standard good like wheat. It is selling a promise to be repaid, and the quality of that promise depends on who is borrowing and what they do with the money. The interest rate is therefore not only a price. It also changes who comes to the bank and what they do once they have the loan. If a higher rate brings worse customers or worse behaviour, the bank’s profit can fall as the rate rises, and the bank rationally stops raising it even though customers are queueing.\n\n7.2 What exactly is “credit rationing”?\nBe careful with the definition, because examiners like it. The authors reserve the term for two cases (pp. 394–395):"}, {"pdf": 77, "printed": 50, "ch": 7, "text": "(a) Among loan applicants who appear identical, some get a loan and some do\nnot, and the rejected ones would not get one even if they offered a higher\ninterest rate.\n(b) There are identifiable groups who cannot get loans at any interest rate with the given supply of credit, though they would with a larger supply. This is what is called redlining (Section 7.5). A bank charging a higher rate to a riskier borrower is not rationing in this sense. It is ordinary risk pricing. The earlier literature the authors mention explained why each borrower faces an upward-sloping rate schedule (larger loans are riskier, for example). This paper claims to supply “the first theoretical justification of true credit rationing” (p. 394).\n\n7.3 The model in four pieces\nPiece 1: borrowers differ in risk, and the bank cannot see it\nEach borrower has a project with a random gross return R. Projects differ in a risk index θ, higher θ meaning riskier in the technical sense of a mean-preserving spread: the same average return but more spread out (p. 395). The bank can tell projects with different means apart, but cannot observe the risk θ of any particular project. It lends an amount B at interest rate r. The borrower has limited liability: if the return R plus any collateral C is smaller than the amount owed, B(1 + r), the borrower defaults and the bank takes R + C. Quantity\n\nFormula (their numbering)\n\nIn words\n\nBorrower’s payoff\n\nπ(R, r) = max[R−(1+r)B, −C] Keeps the surplus if the project suc(4a)\nceeds; loses at most the collateral if it\nfails.\n\nBank’s payoff\n\nρ(R, r) = min[R + C, B(1 + r)] Gets the promised amount, or what(4b)\never the project and collateral can\npay.\n\nTable 7.1. The payoffs that drive everything (p. 396). The borrower’s payoff is convex in R and the bank’s is concave in R.\n\nWhy convex and concave matters. A convex payoff (like a call option) gains from greater spread; a concave payoff loses from it. So a riskier project with the same mean is worth more to the borrower and less to the bank. This single asymmetry is the engine of the whole paper. It is the same logic as the equity-versus-debt asymmetry in finance: the borrower holds the upside and the bank holds the capped downside.\n\nPiece 2: the interest rate as a screen (adverse selection)\nTheorem 1 (p. 396): at a given r, there is a critical risk level θ̂ such that a firm applies if and only if θ > θ̂. The reason is the convexity above: riskier firms value the loan more. Theorem 2: as r rises, θ̂ rises. At a higher rate the safest borrowers drop out first, so the average applicant is riskier."}, {"pdf": 78, "printed": 51, "ch": 7, "text": "Theorem 3: the bank’s expected return falls with the riskiness of the loan (concavity). Put them together and you get two forces on the bank’s profit as it raises r: a direct effect (more money per repaid loan, which raises profit) and an adverse selection effect (a worse pool, which lowers it). If the second is stronger beyond some rate, the bank’s expected return per rupee lent, ρ(r), rises, peaks at a “bank-optimal” rate r∗ , then falls (Figure 7.1). Theorem 4 (p. 397) shows non-monotonicity is guaranteed if borrowers come in discrete types: each time the next safer group drops out, ρ(r) jumps down. The authors also give smoother sufficient conditions (pp. 399–400): two-outcome projects where the bank suffers a deadweight loss X per dollar from default (the sufficient conditions compare X with the gap between the best success return K and the failure value D, and depend on how thinly the top projects are populated), and a case where a share of borrowers is very risk averse and so only takes safe projects. Market: rationing at r * 2.00\n\n1.15\n\nloan demand\n\n1.75\n\n1.10\nLoans (index)\n\nBank's expected return per rupee ρ(r)\n\nr * : bank return\n\nReturn to the bank is highest here\n\n1.05\n1.00\n0.95\n\n1.50\n1.25\n1.00\n\n0.90\n\n0.75\n\n0.85\n\n0.50\n\nexcess demand Z\nat r *\n\nloan supply (follows ρ)\n\n0.25\n\nInterest rate r (%)\n\nInterest rate r (%)\n\nFigure 7.1. Left: the bank’s expected return ρ(r) per unit lent peaks at r∗ , because safe borrowers leave as the rate rises. Right: loan demand falls with r; loan supply depends on the return ρ banks earn. At r∗ demand exceeds supply, yet no bank gains by raising the rate. rm is the rate at which demand would equal supply but it is not an equilibrium. The curves are my own numerical illustration built on the structure of the model (Theorem 5, Figure 4 of the paper); they are not reproduced from the paper.\n\nPiece 3: the equilibrium with rationing\nTheorem 5 (p. 397): whenever ρ(r) has an interior maximum, there exist supply functions of funds such that the competitive equilibrium has credit rationing. The logic is Figure 7.1. Competition among banks pushes the rate to r∗ , because a bank that charges r∗ attracts at least the same borrowers as at higher rates and earns more per loan. A bank that offers a rate above r∗ loses money on the extra risk. A rejected borrower who offers to pay more is not welcomed: “The bank would not lend to an individual who offered to pay more than r∗ . In the bank’s judgment, such a loan is likely to be a worse risk than the average loan at interest rate r∗ ” (p. 394). The market-clearing rate rm exists, but “rm is not an equilibrium interest rate” (p. 398). Corollary 1 (p. 398) has a policy consequence worth remembering: if the supply of funds rises, the interest rate does not fall while rationing persists. Only the amount of excess demand"}, {"pdf": 79, "printed": 52, "ch": 7, "text": "shrinks. When excess demand reaches zero, further increases in supply finally lower the rate. Theorem 6: when ρ(r) has several local peaks, there can be a two-rate equilibrium, with some of the rationed borrowers able to obtain credit at the higher rate (pp. 398–399). This is a technical point you can skip.\n\nPiece 4: the interest rate as an incentive (moral hazard)\nThe second channel (Section II, pp. 401–402) does not depend on hidden borrower types. Here a given borrower chooses between projects, and the bank cannot see the choice. Theorem 7: if a risk-neutral firm is indifferent between two projects, a higher r makes it prefer the one with the higher probability of bankruptcy. Theorem 8: the bank’s return falls with r if, at that rate, the firm is indifferent between a riskier and a safer project. The idea is that raising the rate reduces the borrower’s profit when things go well but not when they go badly (limited liability protects her), so the relative attraction of the high-risk, high-payoff project increases. Success prob.\n\nReturn\nsuccess\n\nif Expected return\n\nFirm’s payoff at\nr = 20%\n\nSafe project (b)\n\n0.90\n\n1.90\n\n1.71\n\n0.9(1.90 − 1.20) =\n0.63\n\nRisky project (a)\n\n0.50\n\n2.40\n\n1.20\n\n0.5(2.40 − 1.20) =\n0.60\n\nTable 7.2. Worked example (my numbers, using the structure of the paper’s Section II.B, pp. 401–402). One rupee is borrowed; the borrower provides no collateral. At 20% the firm prefers the safe project.\n\nFrom equation (16) of the paper the firm is indifferent when (1 + r∗ ) =\n\npb R b − p a R a\n. Here this\npb − pa\n\n1.71 − 1.20\n= 1.275, so r∗ = 27.5%. Below 27.5% the firm chooses the safe project; above it, 0.40 the risky one. The bank’s return per rupee lent is therefore: Rate r\n\nProject chosen Bank’s expected\nper rupee lent\n\nreturn Comment\n\n10%\n\nsafe\n\n0.9 × 1.10 = 0.990\n\nBelow the bank’s cost of funds (1.00).\n\n20%\n\nsafe\n\n0.9 × 1.20 = 1.080\n\nProfitable.\n\n27.5%\n\nsafe (indifferent) 0.9 × 1.275 = 1.1475\n\n30%\n\nrisky\n\n0.5 × 1.30 = 0.650\n\nCollapses: the firm switches.\n\n50%\n\nrisky\n\n0.5 × 1.50 = 0.750\n\nStill far below the peak.\n\nMaximum for the bank.\n\nTable 7.3. The bank’s return is hump-shaped (my calculation from the numbers in Table 7.2). A bank facing excess demand at 27.5% gains nothing by raising the rate to 30% or 50%.\n\nThe paper adds a striking welfare remark (p. 408): if the safe project has higher expected return than the risky one (as in the numbers above: 1.71 against 1.20) but the maximum rate at which"}, {"pdf": 80, "printed": 53, "ch": 7, "text": "borrowers stay safe is too low for the bank, then the bank may choose a rate that induces only risky projects. “In this case a usury law forbidding interest rates in excess of r∗ will increase net national output.” That is, a price ceiling can improve efficiency. Do not misread this as a general case for ceilings; it is a result of the particular assumptions.\n\n7.4 Collateral does not cure it\nAn obvious objection: if a bank has excess demand, why not demand more collateral or equity instead of raising the rate? Section III (pp. 402–406) says collateral requirements have their own adverse selection and incentive effects. The key results are:\n• Theorem 9: a contract {C, r} screens borrowers by wealth.\n• Theorem 10: with decreasing absolute risk aversion, wealthier individuals undertake riskier projects. (The rich can afford to gamble; those who can put up the most collateral are the least risk averse.)\n• Theorem 11: collateral raises the bank’s return from any given borrower.\n• Theorem 12: but raising the collateral requirement drives out the less wealthy, more cautious borrowers, so the average borrower who remains is riskier. This selection effect can outweigh the direct benefit (Figure 8, p. 405). There is also an adverse incentive effect of cutting loan size (pp. 405–406): if borrowers financed with less up front respond by taking actions that require more bank lending later (for example, skimping on back-up systems), a smaller initial loan can actually lower the bank’s profit.\n\n7.5 Redlining: Theorems 13 and 14\nWhen borrowers fall into observably different groups, each with its own bank-optimal rate, competition equalises the bank’s best achievable return across the groups that get loans, and groups whose best return falls below the cost of funds get no loans at any rate. That is redlining (pp. 406–407). The paper makes the important qualification that “there is no presumption that the market equilibrium allocates credit to those for whom the expected return on their investments is highest” (p. 407): the excluded group may have the highest social return and the bank still cannot profit from it.\n\n7.6 Beyond credit: the principal-agent generalisation\nSection V (pp. 407–408) points out that the same logic applies to landlords and tenants, and to employers and employees: any contract where the price affects the quality of the transaction. They argue that neither pure debt nor pure equity is efficient, so one should expect mixed, non-linear contracts. The Conclusions (pp. 408–409) add two things:\n• Excess supply equilibria are also possible. A bank that tries to poach a rival’s customers gets only the ones the rival is happy to lose, so banks hesitate to cut rates (p. 409)."}, {"pdf": 81, "printed": 54, "ch": 7, "text": "• Monetary policy works through availability of credit, not the interest rate. “to the extent that monetary policy succeeds in shifting the supply of funds, it will affect the level of investment, not through the interest rate mechanism, but rather through the availability of credit” (p. 409). The authors note this is a “monetarist” result with a different mechanism. The last paragraph states the moral: “The Law of Supply and Demand is not in fact a law, nor should it be viewed as an assumption needed for competitive analysis. It is rather a result generated by the underlying assumptions that prices have neither sorting nor incentive effects” (p. 409).\n\n7.7 Common misreadings\nFive things the paper did not say\n\n1. “Credit is always rationed.” The authors write: “It is not our argument that credit rationing will always characterize capital markets, but rather that it may occur under not implausible assumptions” (p. 394).\n2. “Rationing is caused by rules or sticky rates.” The result is for a frictionless, competitive market. The cause is information, not rigidity.\n3. “Rationing means poor or unsound borrowers are refused.” Rationing means\nobservationally identical applicants are treated differently. The refused applicant may be as sound as the accepted one.\n4. “The paper is only about adverse selection.” It has three mechanisms: selection by interest rate (Section I), incentives from the interest rate (Section II), and selection and incentives from collateral (Section III).\n5. “Asymmetric information means Knightian uncertainty.” It does not. In this\nmodel everyone knows the distributions F (R, θ). What is hidden is which distribution a given borrower has. That is a problem of information about a known kind of risk, not an absence of any basis for probability. See the thesis section below.\n\n7.8 A note on later criticism\nThis section draws on general knowledge, not on your files; check it before citing. Two criticisms are standard. First, Hart (1985), cited by Tirole in his footnote 1 (p. 114, your Tirole file), observed that the project-risk conflict could be removed by profit-sharing contracts instead of debt; Tirole’s own model avoids this by using a different moral hazard (shirking for private benefit). Second, de Meza and Webb (1987) showed that under a different assumption about who is hidden-type the market can instead overlend. Neither undoes the paper’s point that rationing is possible in equilibrium; they show it depends on assumptions. Chapter 8 carries on with the moral-hazard version.\n\n7.9 What it means for the thesis\nThis section is my inference about the thesis, not the authors’ claim."}, {"pdf": 82, "printed": 55, "ch": 7, "text": "A mechanism for why banks may ration rather than reprice transition risk. If a public sector bank is exposed to a high-emissions sector and knows that raising the rate on those borrowers could attract riskier projects or push existing ones to riskier behaviour, the model predicts quantity limits (exposure caps, sector limits) and not higher spreads. This fits the NGFS-type scenario in which transition risk raises the dispersion of outcomes across firms in the same sector. A link between collateral and the climate shock. Collateral values (land, plant, mines) may fall when policy makes assets stranded. Theorem 12 and the incentive effect (p. 405) say a bank that responds by demanding more collateral may worsen its pool. The thesis can frame the effect of collateral devaluation on lending as a test of whether credit supply, not demand, responds to transition exposure. A limit you should state. The paper treats risk as a known distribution. Transition risk, as Phase 1 argued, is partly Type 3 uncertainty. The paper therefore gives you a story about information frictions, not about the unmeasurability of climate outcomes. The two stories are complements, not substitutes, and a thesis that says so is stronger. An institutional caution. In India, interest rates on many public sector bank loans are linked to external benchmarks or priority-sector rules, so rationing in practice may arise partly from regulation and not only from the mechanism here. The paper is a theory of the case with no such regulation. Do you need the original? For the argument: no. This chapter covers all the theorems you are likely to be asked about. Open the original in these cases:\n• You plan to cite a specific theorem number or quote the definition of rationing. Check pp. 394–395 (definition), p. 397 (Theorem 5 and Figure 4), p. 401 (Theorems 7–8), pp. 403–405 (Theorems 9–12).\n• You plan to claim something about the proofs or the sufficient conditions. The conditions on pp. 399–400 are only described in words here; read them in the paper.\n• You want the Figure 4 four-quadrant diagram. It is hard to describe; look at p. 397. Everything else (the later parts on redlining and the principal-agent view) is covered here.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ7.1 Give the paper’s two definitions of credit rationing. Which one is redlining?\nQ7.2 In one sentence each, state Theorems 1, 2 and 3.\nQ7.3 What is the “bank-optimal” interest rate, and why is it not the market-clearing rate?\nQ7.4 According to Corollary 1, what happens to the interest rate when the supply of funds increases while rationing exists? Explain"}, {"pdf": 83, "printed": 56, "ch": 7, "text": "Q7.5 Why does a mean-preserving spread make a project more valuable to the borrower and less valuable to the bank? Use the shapes in Table 7.1.\nQ7.6 In Table 7.3 the bank’s return at r = 30% is lower than at r = 20%. Explain the whole chain from the interest rate to the bank’s return, naming the effect.\nQ7.7 Why does the paper say raising collateral requirements can reduce the bank’s return? Distinguish the direct effect from the selection effect.\nQ7.8 The authors say the law of supply and demand is “not a law”. Under what assumption would it hold? Apply\nQ7.9 Design a numerical example like Table 7.2 in which the safe project is chosen up to r∗ = 15%. (Hint: use 1 + r∗ = (pb Rb − pa Ra )/(pb − pa ).)\nQ7.10 A bank is considering raising the lending rate on a group of thermal-power borrowers whose revenues depend on policy. State which of the paper’s three mechanisms could make this unprofitable, and what observable signs you would look for in loan data.\nQ7.11 How would you tell, empirically, whether a fall in lending to a sector is caused by rationing or by fall in demand? What does the paper imply about the interest rate in each case? Viva\nQ7.12 An examiner says: “Your thesis uses the credit-rationing literature, but in India lending rates are often set by regulation, so rationing is just regulation.” Give a reply of three sentences.\nQ7.13 “Stiglitz–Weiss is about risk, but your thesis says climate is uncertainty. Isn’t that inconsistent?” How do you answer without dropping either?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA7.1 (a) Among apparently identical applicants some get loans and rejected ones would not get one even at a higher interest rate; (b) identifiable groups cannot borrow at any rate given the supply of credit, though they could with more supply. (b) is redlining.\nA7.2 T1: for a given r there is a critical risk θ̂ such that a firm borrows iff θ > θ̂. T2: θ̂ rises with r. T3: the bank’s expected return falls as loan riskiness rises.\nA7.3 The r that maximises the bank’s expected return per unit lent, ρ(r). It differs from rm (where demand equals supply) because beyond r∗ higher rates worsen the pool or the behaviour enough to lower ρ.\nA7.4 The rate stays at r∗ ; only excess demand falls, until it reaches zero.\nA7.5 The borrower’s payoff is convex in R (she keeps the upside, loses little on the downside), so spread helps; the bank’s is concave (capped upside, exposed downside), so spread hurts.\nA7.6 Direct effect: more income per repaid rupee. Adverse incentive effect (Theorem 8):"}, {"pdf": 84, "printed": 57, "ch": 7, "text": "the higher rate makes the firm switch to the risky project, with success probability 0.5 instead of 0.9, and this outweighs the higher rate.\nA7.7 Direct: collateral raises the return from any given borrower. Selection: the less wealthy, less risk-tolerant borrowers drop out, so the rest are riskier; beyond a critical collateral the second effect dominates.\nA7.8 When prices have no sorting or incentive effects, i.e., when the quality of the thing traded does not depend on the price.\nA7.9 Example: take pb = 0.9, pa = 0.5. Need pb Rb − pa Ra = 0.4 × 1.15 = 0.46. With Ra = 2.4 (pa Ra = 1.20), pb Rb = 1.66, so Rb = 1.844. Check the firm is indifferent at r = 15%.\nA7.10 Adverse selection (safer thermal borrowers leave), incentive (borrower switches to riskier operations to earn the return needed to pay the rate), and collateral effects. Signs: rising non-performing assets after rate increases, declining share of high-rated borrowers in the applicant pool, and refusals of applicants who offer higher rates.\nA7.11 Under demand fall, the quoted rate would fall and approval rates among applicants stay stable; under rationing, rates stay flat or sticky while approvals fall and applicants who look identical are treated differently. The paper implies that in the second case the interest rate does not move to clear the market.\nA7.12 Regulation can explain a cap, but the paper’s rationing arises in an unregulated competitive market. Regulated rates may add to it. The thesis should therefore test for rationing controlling for rate rules and say which source dominates.\nA7.13 Different layers. S–W’s risk is a known distribution with hidden types, an information problem; climate outcomes add Type 3 uncertainty about the distributions themselves. Both can hold: information frictions amplify the effect of uncertainty on credit supply."}, {"pdf": 85, "printed": 58, "ch": 8, "text": "CHAPTER 8\n\nTirole (2006), Chapter 3: Outside Financing Capacity\nSource line\nJean Tirole, The Theory of Corporate Finance (Princeton University Press, 2006), Chapter 3, “Outside Financing Capacity”, printed pp. 113–149 (text pp. 113–129, supplementary models pp. 130–144, exercises after that). Your file is the whole book; this chapter covers the text of Chapter 3 and signposts the rest. Page numbers are the book’s printed pages. A correction to the reading plan\n\nThe plan calls Chapter 3 “Adverse Selection” and Chapter 5 “Credit Rationing”. In your copy, Chapter 3 is the credit rationing chapter (through moral hazard), Chapter 5 is Liquidity and Risk Management, and adverse selection is Chapter 6. This chapter covers Chapter 3. I have not summarised Chapters 5 and 6 here: ask if you want them, and the thesis does not need them unless you plan to use the liquidity or adverse-selection versions.\n\n8.1 Where the chapter sits\nTirole’s book builds one simple model and then asks it questions. Chapter 3 introduces that model. Later chapters add diversification (Ch. 4), liquidity (Ch. 5), and so on; the macrofinance applications are at the end (Chapters 13 and 14). If you understand the model of this chapter you understand the engine of the whole book, and the numbers ρ0 and ρ1 below come back repeatedly.\n\n8.2 The puzzle and Tirole’s view of Stiglitz–Weiss\nTirole opens with a quotation from Bester and Hellwig (1987): a would-be borrower is rationed “if he cannot obtain the loan that he wants even though he is willing to pay the interest that the lenders are asking, perhaps even a higher interest” (p. 113). He notes that interest ceilings are mostly gone and rationing remains, and credits Jaffee–Russell (1976), Keeton (1979) and Stiglitz–Weiss (1981) with turning rationing into an equilibrium phenomenon (previous chapter). Both explanations (moral hazard and adverse selection) begin from the same observation: with limited liability, a higher interest rate “has no effect on the borrower in the event of bankruptcy” but lowers her income when the project succeeds, so it reduces her stake in the project (p. 113)."}, {"pdf": 86, "printed": 59, "ch": 8, "text": "Stiglitz–Weiss (1981)\nHidden thing\n\nTirole, Chapter 3\n\nBorrower’s project risk (type or Borrower’s effort or behaviour: does\nchoice).\nshe work, or take a private benefit?\n\nWhy a higher rate Safe borrowers leave;\nhurts the bank\nswitch to risky projects.\n\nsurvivors The higher the borrower’s repayment,\nthe smaller her stake and the weaker\nher incentive to behave.\n\nKey variable\n\nInterest rate r and collateral C.\n\nBorrower’s net worth A and the\nagency cost.\n\nContract form\n\nStandard debt.\n\nProfit-sharing claim, which can be\nread as debt or equity (p. 119).\n\nOutput\n\nA hump-shaped bank return; ra- A minimum net worth Ā; borrowing\ntioning at r∗ .\ncapacity I ≤ kA.\n\nFootnote on the link Tirole’s footnote 1 (p. 113): Hart\n(1985) argued that the S–W risk conflict could be solved by profit sharing, so he uses a different moral hazard. Table 8.1. Two routes to credit rationing. Tirole’s route is easier to extend to a whole economy because net worth appears explicitly.\n\n8.3 The fixed-investment model (Section 3.2, pp. 115–118)\nCast and assumptions\n• An entrepreneur (“she”) has a project that requires a fixed investment I. She has own cash or net worth A < I and so must borrow I − A.\n• The project either succeeds and yields verifiable income R > 0, or fails and yields 0. The success probability is pH if she behaves (no private benefit) and pL = pH − ∆p < pH if she misbehaves, in which case she enjoys a private benefit B > 0.\n• Everyone is risk neutral, the riskless rate is 0, lenders compete and break even, and the borrower has limited liability (p. 116).\n• The project is worth doing only if she behaves: pH R − I > 0 (3.1) but pL R − I + B < 0 (3.2). So lenders must make sure she behaves.\n\nStep 1: the incentive constraint\nThe loan contract splits success income R into Rb for the borrower and Rl for lenders (0 each if the project fails). She behaves if she prefers pH Rb to pL Rb + B: (∆p) Rb ≥ B\n\n(ICb ).\n\nThe most she can be left with is not less than B/∆p. So the most that can be promised to lenders is R − B/∆p, and the pledgeable income is B \u0011 P = pH R −"}, {"pdf": 87, "printed": 60, "ch": 8, "text": "Step 2: the lenders’ break-even constraint and the minimum net worth\nLenders will lend only if pledgeable income covers what they put in: P ≥ I − A. Rearranged, financing requires A ≥ Ā = pH − (pH R − I) (3.3, p. 117). The term pH B/∆p is the agency rent: the minimum expected monetary payoff the borrower must keep to be motivated. The term pH R − I is the project’s net present value (NPV). The model is interesting only if NPV is smaller than the agency rent, i.e., Ā > 0 (3.4). The central result A project with positive NPV may not be financed, if the borrower’s net worth is below Ā. “With insufficient assets, the entrepreneur must borrow a large amount and therefore pledge a large fraction of the return in the case of success. The entrepreneur then keeps only a small fraction of the monetary gain and is demotivated” (p. 117). This is credit rationing: a rationed borrower “may be willing to give a high fraction of the return to the lenders, which here is equivalent to being willing to pay a high interest rate. But the lenders do not want to grant such a loan” (p. 117).\n\nWorked example\nAssumed numbers (my choice; Rs crore) Derived quantities\nI = 100, R = 150, pH = 0.8, pL = 0.5 (∆p = NPV = pH R − I = 20. Misbehaviour check 0.3), B = 15 (3.2): pL R + B − I = 75 + 15 − 100 = −10 < 0. Pledgeable income P = 0.8 (150 − 15/0.3) = Agency rent = pH B/∆p = 0.8 × 50 = 40.\n0.8 × 100 = 80\nMinimum net worth Ā = 40 − 20 = 20\n\nAt A = 20: loan 80, lenders’ claim Rl =\n80/0.8 = 100, borrower keeps Rb = 50;\n∆p Rb = 15 = B ✓.\n\nBorrower with A = 15: loan 85, Rl = 106.25, ∆p Rb = 13.1 < B = 15: she would shirk, so no Rb = 43.75 lender will lend. Rate of interest: 1 + ι = 1/pH = 1.25\n\nA 25% rate on a riskless rate of 0 is a pure\ndefault premium.\n\nTable 8.2. A numerical walk-through of the fixed-investment model. The borrower with A = 20 gets credit; the borrower with A = 15, whose project is equally good, does not."}, {"pdf": 88, "printed": 61, "ch": 8, "text": "Funded only above Ā\n\nHigher agency cost, lower multiplier\n\n160\nMaximum investment I = kA\n\nBorrower's net payoff\n\nNPV = 20 once A ≥ Ā\n\nĀ = 20\n\nBorrower's net worth A\n\n120\n100\n\nB = 0.20, k = 3.00\n\nB = 0.22, k = 2.59\n\nB = 0.15, k = 5.00\n\n140\n\nNet worth A\n\nFigure 8.1. Left: the borrower’s net payoff jumps from 0 to the project’s NPV at the threshold Ā. This follows equation (3.6), p. 118, with the numbers of Table 8.2. Right: the cap on investment I ≤ kA (Section 3.4) for three levels of the borrower’s private benefit B per unit of investment, with k = 1/(1 − ρ0 ). My numerical illustration.\n\nWhat drives rationing\nTwo things, stated on p. 117–118: (i) a low amount of cash on hand A; and (ii) a high agency cost, which depends on the private benefit B and on the likelihood ratio ∆p/pH = (pH −pL )/pH , a measure of how informative success or failure is about effort. A higher ratio means better performance measurement and easier access to finance. Tirole adds that the law and corporate governance affect B: countries with strong investor protection “exhibit lower agency costs” (p. 118). A third factor is the interest rate itself: footnote 13 (p. 117) notes that raising the market rate r is equivalent to lowering cash on hand. In plain words: “one only lends to the rich”\n\nThe borrower has to have enough of her own money in the project that she would lose a lot if she cheated. That is not a moral test. It is arithmetic: the share of income she can safely promise to lenders is limited by the share she must keep to stay motivated, and this limits how much lenders can lend.\n\n8.4 Extensions in Section 3.2 (pp. 119–124)\nDebt or equity? (3.2.3, p. 119). In this two-outcome model the lenders’ claim can be called either risky debt or outside equity; the model does not distinguish. Tirole calls this “both a weakness and a strength”: it lets him study ideas without committing to a form of finance. When a leftover value in failure is added (Section 3.4.3, p. 128) debt priority becomes optimal. Dilution and overborrowing (3.2.4, pp. 119–121). If the borrower can raise new money for a worthless “deepening” investment, she may do so even though initial lenders keep their formal claim, because the new claims alter her incentives. Condition: [pH − (pL + τ )]Rl > C1 + C2 , where C1 is the direct cost and C2 the lost incentive. This is why lenders insist on"}, {"pdf": 89, "printed": 62, "ch": 8, "text": "negative covenants, and why weak borrowers are more prone to overborrow.\nReputation (3.2.5, p. 121). A good reputation reduces the borrower’s private benefit B from misbehaving, which lowers Ā. This is why track record can substitute for cash. Using information (3.2.6, p. 122). Better performance measurement (a more informative signal of effort) reduces agency cost. Investment–cash-flow sensitivity (3.2.7, p. 123). Constrained firms invest more when they have more cash. Tirole warns that the sensitivity is better studied in a dynamic model (Chapter 5).\n\n8.5 Debt overhang (Section 3.3, pp. 125–126)\nFollowing Myers (1977), a borrower is said to suffer from debt overhang when old debts stop her raising new funds for a profitable project. Tirole gives two readings.\n1. Reduced net worth. The borrower has cash or collateral A and owes D to initial investors who hold her assets as collateral. If A > Ā > A − D ≥ 0 the project would be financed without the old debt but is not undertaken with it, because new investors cannot recover their cost plus the old claim (p. 125).\n2. Failed renegotiation. Even a very profitable project cannot be financed if the old senior debt D is large and the old creditors will not forgive part of it. Tirole notes that renegotiation breakdown is the core of Myers’s original analysis (p. 126).\n\n8.6 Borrowing capacity: the equity multiplier (Section 3.4, pp. 127–\n130)\nNow let investment be continuous, with constant returns: investment I ∈ [0, ∞) yields RI on success. The private benefit from misbehaving is BI. With cash A the borrower can invest I and borrow I − A. The same two constraints yield I ≤ kA,\n\n1 − ρ0\n1 − pH R − B/∆p\n\n(3.11–3.12),\n\nwhere ρ1 ≡ pH R is the expected payoff per unit of investment and ρ0 ≡ pH R − pH B/∆p is the pledgeable income per unit. Assumptions guarantee ρ1 > 1 > ρ0 . The key properties (p. 128):\n• k > 1: the borrower can lever her wealth. k is the equity multiplier; debt capacity per unit of net worth is d = ρ0 /(1 − ρ0 ) = k − 1.\n• k is smaller when the private benefit B is higher or the likelihood ratio is lower: a higher agency cost gives a lower multiplier.\n• The shadow value of equity is v = (ρ1 − ρ0 )/(1 − ρ0 ) > 1: one more unit of the borrower’s own cash is worth more than one unit to her because it unlocks borrowing.\n• The borrower’s net utility is (ρ1 − 1)I, so she invests as much as she is allowed."}, {"pdf": 90, "printed": 63, "ch": 8, "text": "Per-unit assumptions: R = 1.5,\npH = 0.8, pL = 0.5\n\nρ0 k = 1−ρ\nd=k−1\n\nB = 0.15 (low agency cost)\n\n0.800\n\n5.00\n\n4.00 2.00\n\nB = 0.20\n\n0.667\n\n3.00\n\n2.00 1.60\n\nB = 0.22 (high agency cost)\n\n0.613\n\n2.59\n\n1.59 1.52\n\nTable 8.3. Equity multiplier and shadow value for three agency costs (my calculation from (3.12)–(3.15)). With ρ1 = pH R = 1.2, the conditions ρ1 > 1 > ρ0 and pL R + B < 1 (3.8) hold in every row. Compare the right-hand panel of Figure 8.1.\n\nRead the table slowly. With a low agency cost a firm with cash 10 can invest 50; with the highest agency cost in the table only about 26. If its cash falls by 2, investment falls by 10 in the first case and by about 5 in the last. Note also how sharply k responds: the private benefit rises from 0.15 to 0.22 per unit (under 50%) and the multiplier halves. This is an amplification mechanism, which is why this chapter feeds directly into the macro-finance chapters (13–14) and into Stiglitz (2011), Chapter 10.\n\nCollateral and the “maximal incentives” principle (3.4.3, p. 128)\nIf the project yields a positive amount RF I even in failure (a salvage value, larger when secondary asset markets are liquid), the indeterminacy between debt and equity disappears: investors should have priority in failure and the entrepreneur should be the residual claimant. In other words, standard debt is derived, not assumed (the chapter’s own preview, p. 115: “it is optimal for investors to have priority in the case of default and for the entrepreneur to be the residual claimant”).\n\n8.7 The rest of the chapter: what you can skip\nSections 3.5–3.8 (pp. 130–144) review three classic alternative models that produce standard debt contracts: verifiable income (maximal insider incentives), semi-verifiable income (Townsend’s costly state verification, with an audit cost K) and non-verifiable income (Bolton– Scharfstein, Hart–Moore, where repayment is motivated by the threat of termination). Tirole says these are not unimportant, only more technical (p. 130). Section 3.9 has exercises. For the thesis you need only the idea that several micro-foundations lead to the same lesson.\n\n8.8 Common misreadings\nFive things the chapter did not say\n\n1. “Net worth is just collateral.” In the main model A is cash or liquid assets used to finance the project (p. 115); collateral enters properly in Section 3.4.3.\n2. “Rationed borrowers are bad projects.” Rationed projects have positive NPV. That is the point."}, {"pdf": 91, "printed": 64, "ch": 8, "text": "3. “The interest rate is 0 in reality.” The riskless rate is normalised to zero; the nominal rate is 1/pH − 1, a default premium (p. 116).\n4. “The model proves banks should hold more capital.” It is a model of nonfinancial borrowers. Application to banks requires extra steps (see below and Stiglitz 2011, pp. 614–615).\n5. “Debt and equity are the same.” Only in the two-outcome case; the book later distinguishes them by priority (Section 3.4.3) and by control rights (Chapter 10).\n\n8.9 What it means for the thesis\nThis section is my inference about the thesis, not Tirole’s claim.\nThe formal parallel with capital adequacy. The inequality I ≤ kA has the same structure as a capital requirement: lending is limited to a multiple of own funds, with the multiple set by how much outsiders trust the insider’s incentives. For a bank, the regulator’s minimum ratio plays a role similar to 1/k. Lavoie (Chapter 12) reports the argument, made by some economists, that a maximum ratio of assets to own funds “may replace the role of reserve multipliers”, and then gives reasons to doubt that it binds. Climate shocks as a hit to A and a rise in B. If transition policy lowers the value of a borrower’s assets (stranded assets), A falls. If it increases opacity or tempts firms to delay change, effective B may rise. Both reduce borrowing capacity. This gives a theory of why a brown firm’s viable green investments may still be unfunded, without assuming that the investments are poor. Debt overhang and the green transition. A firm with large legacy debt may be unable to fund a profitable green retrofit because the new money would mostly pay off old creditors (Section 3.3). This suggests a testable idea: legacy leverage should reduce transition capex more strongly where covenants are tight. A limit. The model has verifiable income and a single hidden action by an individual entrepreneur. It has no systemic risk and no regulation. It explains the micro logic of constraints; Chapter 10 explains the macro amplification. Do you need the original? For the model: no. Sections 3.2 and 3.4 are fully reproduced here, with the key formulas. Open the original in these cases:\n• You will quote or derive (3.3), (3.12) or (3.15). Check pp. 116–118 and 127–128.\n• You want the dilution proof or the debt-overhang renegotiation model. Read pp. 119– 121 and 125–126.\n• You will use costly state verification or the Hart–Moore models. Read the supplementary sections 3.5–3.8 (pp. 130–144); I have only named them.\n• You want the macro applications. Read Chapters 13 and 14; they are not summarised here."}, {"pdf": 92, "printed": 65, "ch": 8, "text": "Check yourself\nRecall\nQ8.1 State the incentive constraint (ICb ) and the pledgeable income. Which is the quantity that limits borrowing?\nQ8.2 Write the threshold Ā and name its two parts.\nQ8.3 Define the equity multiplier k in terms of ρ0 . What happens to k when the private benefit rises?\nQ8.4 Name two reasons that, according to Tirole, a firm may be credit constrained. Explain\nQ8.5 In Table 8.2, why can the entrepreneur with A = 15 not simply promise to repay a higher interest rate to get the loan?\nQ8.6 Why is the shadow value of equity greater than 1? What would happen to it if the project were riskless?\nQ8.7 Why does an extra source of finance for a “deepening” investment hurt initial lenders even though their formal claim is untouched?\nQ8.8 Compare the roles of the interest rate in Stiglitz–Weiss and in Tirole. Where does each appear in the mechanism? Apply\nQ8.9 Using R = 1.4, pH = 0.85, pL = 0.55, B = 0.15 per unit, compute ρ0 , k and d. Check that ρ1 > 1 > ρ0 and that pL R + B < 1.\nQ8.10 A borrower has A = 100 and k = 3. A shock cuts her net worth by 20%. What happens to her maximum investment? What if k = 6?\nQ8.11 A bank lends to a coal-power firm that owes legacy debt D. State, in terms of A, Ā and D, when the firm cannot fund a profitable solar addition (Section 3.3.1). Viva\nQ8.12 “Tirole assumes risk neutrality and zero interest. How can that say anything about banks under Basel norms?” Answer in four sentences.\nQ8.13 “If rationed projects are positive NPV, why don’t other lenders step in?” Give the model’s answer.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA8.1 (∆p)Rb ≥ B; pledgeable income P = pH (R − B/∆p). Pledgeable income limits borrowing because lenders can receive at most this.\nA8.2 Ā = pH B/∆p − (pH R − I): the agency rent minus the NPV.\nA8.3 k = 1/(1 − ρ0 ) with ρ0 = pH R − pH B/∆p. A higher B lowers ρ0 and so lowers k."}, {"pdf": 93, "printed": 66, "ch": 8, "text": "A8.4 Low cash on hand; high agency cost (large private benefit and a low likelihood ratio); also a high market interest rate, weak investor protection (any two).\nA8.5 Because a higher promised share leaves her with less than B/∆p in the success state, so she shirks and the lenders’ expected income falls. Her offer is not credible.\nA8.6 Because an extra rupee unlocks k rupees of investment each earning surplus. If the project were riskless in the sense that there were no moral hazard (B = 0), pledgeable income would equal the whole expected return, and equity would be worth only 1.\nA8.7 The new financing changes her incentives (more leverage, less stake) and so lowers the probability of success on the original loan; the lenders lose through the incentive channel even though their share in success is unchanged.\nA8.8 S–W: the rate selects types and induces risk-taking; the hump in the bank’s return gives rationing. Tirole: the rate acts through the borrower’s stake; rationing arises from the net worth constraint; in effect a high rate is equivalent to low cash.\nA8.9 ∆p = 0.30, so B/∆p = 0.5 and ρ0 = 0.85(1.4 − 0.5) = 0.765; k = 1/0.235 ≈ 4.26; d ≈ 3.26. ρ1 = 0.85 × 1.4 = 1.19 > 1 > 0.765 ✓and pL R + B = 0.77 + 0.15 = 0.92 < 1\nA8.10 A 20% fall: A = 80. With k = 3, max investment falls from 300 to 240 (a fall of 60). With k = 6, from 600 to 480 (fall of 120). Higher multiplier, bigger amplification.\nA8.11 If A > Ā but A − D < Ā (with A − D ≥ 0), the solar project is rationed because the legacy claim absorbs the net worth the new lenders need.\nA8.12 Model scope: it concerns an incentive problem, not Basel rules. What carries over is the structure of “lending limited to a multiple of own funds, with the multiple set by agency cost”. A bank’s regulator ratio plays a similar role. Careful claims should say “analogous”, not “identical”.\nA8.13 In this model all lenders face the same agency problem and the same pledgeable income, so a new lender is in the same position as an old one: no one can profitably lend more. The positive NPV is not capturable."}, {"pdf": 94, "printed": 67, "ch": 9, "text": "CHAPTER 9\n\nFama (1970): Efficient Capital Markets\nSource line\nEugene F. Fama, “Efficient Capital Markets: A Review of Theory and Empirical Work”, Journal of Finance 25(2), May 1970, pp. 383–417 (papers and proceedings of the 1969 American Finance Association meeting). Your file is the complete JSTOR copy. Page numbers below are printed journal pages. Fama later wrote a sequel (“Efficient Capital Markets: II”, 1991); it is not in your folder, and what I say about it is general knowledge to be checked.\n\n9.1 What the paper is and why it still matters\nThis is the paper that turned a loose belief (“you cannot beat the market”) into a testable hypothesis. It does two things. First, it states the theory precisely enough that evidence can contradict it. Second, it surveys the evidence up to 1970 and concludes that, “with but a few exceptions, the efficient markets model stands up well” (p. 383). The three-way classification of information sets (weak, semi-strong, strong) is Fama’s most lasting contribution, and it is the vocabulary used in every finance course since. For your thesis the paper matters in three ways: as the core of the orthodox view of finance that Stiglitz (Chapter 10) and Lavoie (Chapters 11 and 12) attack; as the origin of the eventstudy method you may use; and as the source of the habit of testing a model jointly with an assumption about equilibrium returns.\n\n9.2 The core idea in one sentence\nA market is efficient if prices at every moment “fully reflect” all available information (p. 383). The sentence has no testable content by itself: “The definitional statement that in an efficient market prices ‘fully reflect’ available information is so general that it has no empirically testable implications” (p. 383). To test it one must say what “fully reflect” means, and that requires a model of how expected returns are determined. This is the origin of what later became known as the joint-hypothesis problem: every test is a test of efficiency and of the model of expected returns together. In plain words\n\nImagine you could predict tomorrow’s price rise from information everyone has. Then everyone would buy today, and the price would rise today, and the prediction would disappear. If that process works well and quickly, today’s price already contains the information. “Efficient” does not mean prices are always right, only that you cannot use"}, {"pdf": 95, "printed": 68, "ch": 9, "text": "the information set in question to earn more than the normal return for the risk you take.\n\n9.3 The theory in four steps (pp. 384–388)\nStep 1: the expected-return, or “fair game”, model\nLet pj,t be the price of security j at time t, Φt the information assumed to be fully reflected, and rj,t+1 the one-period return. The general model says (his equation 1) E(p̃j,t+1 | Φt ) = [1 + E(r̃j,t+1 | Φt )] pj,t . In words: the expected next-period price, given what is known, equals today’s price grown at the equilibrium expected return, whatever the right theory of that return is (the CAPM of Sharpe and Lintner is one example). Define the surprise xj,t+1 = p̃j,t+1 − E(p̃j,t+1 | Φt ). Then E(xj,t+1 | Φt ) = 0: the sequence of surprises is a fair game with respect to Φt . The implication: no trading system based only on Φt can have expected profit above the equilibrium return. For any trading rule α(Φt ) that allocates funds across securities, the expected excess value it creates is zero (p. 385).\n\nStep 2: the submartingale\nIf the equilibrium expected return is non-negative, the price sequence is a submartingale: expected next price at least equals today’s price (equation 6, p. 386). If the expected return is exactly zero it is a martingale. A submartingale implies that no “one security and cash” trading rule can beat buying and holding on average (p. 386).\n\nStep 3: the random walk\nThe earliest versions assumed that successive returns are independent and identically distributed, which is the random walk (equation 7, p. 386). Fama stresses that the random walk is a special case: it demands more than efficiency does, namely that the whole distribution of returns is independent of the past. “It is best to regard the random walk model as an extension of the general expected return or fair game efficient markets model” (p. 387). Evidence against independence therefore does not automatically refute efficiency.\n\nStep 4: market conditions that help or hinder efficiency\nFama gives sufficient conditions for efficiency: no transactions costs; all information costlessly available to all; all agree on its implications (p. 387). Real markets violate these. But the conditions are not necessary: “even large transactions costs that inhibit the flow of transactions do not in themselves imply that when transactions do take place, prices will not ‘fully reflect’ available information” (p. 388). Disagreement among investors is also not inefficiency, “unless there are investors who can consistently make better evaluations of available information than are implicit in market prices” (p. 388). These are the three frictions he names: transactions costs, information not free to all, and disagreement about implications. They are “potential sources” of inefficiency (p. 388), and measuring their effects is “the major goal of empirical work.”"}, {"pdf": 96, "printed": 69, "ch": 9, "text": "Model\n\nWhat it assumes\n\nStrength\n\nFair game / ex- The conditions of equilibrium can be Weakest. Allows serial correlation in repected return stated in terms of expected returns; the turns as long as it is a change in equilibinformation Φt is fully used. rium expected return. Submartingale\n\nExpected return ≥ 0 given Φt .\n\nRandom walk\n\nSuccessive returns independent and iden- Strongest. Needs the whole distribution tically distributed (p. 386). to be stable and independent.\n\nStronger. Rules out systems beating buyand-hold.\n\nTable 9.1. Fama’s three nested models of price behaviour. Each implies the one above it. Evidence against a strong version does not refute a weaker one.\n\n9.4 The three forms\n\nStrong: all information\nSemi-strong: all public information\n\nWeak: past\nprices only\n\nEach larger set contains the smaller: if prices reflect the larger set they reflect the smaller.\n\nFigure 9.1. The three information sets are nested. A market that is efficient in the strong form is also efficient in the semi-strong and weak forms, not the reverse. My diagram of the classification in Fama (1970), Section III, p. 388; the examples of evidence are from the text.\n\nForm\n\nInformation set Φt Typical test\n\nWeak\n\nPast prices and re- Serial correlations, runs tests, “The results are strongly in supturns. filter rules (pp. 389–397). port” (p. 414).\n\nSemi-strong\n\nAll obviously public Event studies: splits, earnings, Consistent\nwith\nefficiency,\ninformation.\nnew issues (pp. 402–410).\nthough “few different types of\npublic information have been\nexamined” (p. 410).\n\nStrong\n\nspeAll information, in- Do specialists, insiders or Two clear exceptions:\ncluding private.\nmutual funds have an edge? cialists and corporate insiders\n(p. 415).\n(pp. 409–413).\n\nTable 9.2. The three forms of efficiency and the evidence on each.\n\nFama’s verdict"}, {"pdf": 97, "printed": 70, "ch": 9, "text": "9.5 The evidence in plain terms\nWeak form (pp. 389–400)\nSerial correlation. Table 1 of the paper (p. 393) reports first-order serial correlations of daily, four-day, nine-day and sixteen-day changes in log price for the thirty Dow Jones stocks, roughly 1957–62. They are all small. Fama draws four lessons (p. 394). With about 1,200–1,700 daily observations, a correlation as low as 0.06 is more than twice its standard error (≈ 1/ N ), so eleven daily values count as “significant”; twenty-two of the thirty are positive. But 0.06 explains only 0.36% of the variation (0.062 ). Statistical significance is therefore not economic significance: “it is unlikely that the small absolute levels of serial correlation that are always observed can be used as the basis of substantially profitable trading systems” (p. 394). Trading rules. The most famous are filter rules (Alexander; Fama and Blume): buy after a price rise of y%, sell short after a fall of y% from a peak. Large filters cannot beat buy-andhold. Very small filters (under about 1.5%) do beat it before costs, but they trade so often that “minimum trading costs are sufficient to wipe out their advantage”: even a NYSE floor trader pays about 0.1% per round trip (p. 396). Departures that do not break efficiency. Large daily price changes tend to be followed by large changes of random sign (Fama; Osborne): the first-day adjustment is unbiased but incomplete information is still being digested (p. 396). Niederhoffer and Osborne found that price reversals are two to three times as frequent as continuations from trade to trade, which they explained by how market orders meet limit orders on the specialist’s book (pp. 396–397). It refutes the random walk for transaction-to-transaction changes but does not allow a profitable rule (p. 415). The shape of the distribution. Daily price changes have fatter tails than the normal. Mandelbrot suggested stable (non-normal) distributions with infinite variance; Fama’s own testing found these “a better description of distributions of daily returns on common stocks than the normal” (p. 399). Economists were reluctant because “the wealth of statistical techniques available for dealing with normal variables” (p. 400). This matters for risk measurement: fat tails are the origin of the Value-at-Risk and expected-shortfall problems of Phase 5.\n\nSemi-strong form (pp. 402–410)\nThe tool is the market model (equation 12): rj,t+1 = αj + βj rM,t+1 + uj,t+1 . Fama reports (p. 403) that it fits as a linear regression, with parameters stable over long periods and residuals roughly independent. For a public announcement, take the average residual um in month m relative to the event and the cumulative average residual Um = k=−29 uk ; if the market adjusts immediately and without bias, Um should jump around the event and then stay flat. Stock splits (Fama, Fisher, Jensen and Roll). 940 NYSE splits from 1927–59 (p. 405). Cumulative residuals rise for 29 months before the split (firms split after good times) and show no net movement afterwards. Where the split was followed by a dividend increase, they stay flat; where the dividend was cut, they fall back to where they had been about five months before the split (pp. 406–407). The authors read the split as a signal about dividends that the market understood and priced by the end of the split month."}, {"pdf": 98, "printed": 71, "ch": 9, "text": "Earnings announcements (Ball and Brown). For 261 firms (1946–66), cumulative residuals rise throughout the year for firms whose earnings turn out to increase and fall for those that decrease, so that “no more than about ten to fifteen percent of the information in the annual earnings announcement has not been anticipated by the month of the announcement” (p. 408). Waud found a small announcement effect for discount-rate changes (never exceeding 0.5%) and signs that the market anticipates them. Scholes found that secondary offerings are followed by a 1–2% decline that is unrelated to size, so it is information, not “selling pressure” (pp. 408–409). Under-reaction: drift on\n\nCumulative abnormal return (%)\n\nEfficient: jump, then flat\n\nOver-reaction: reversal\n\n−10\n\nDays relative to announcement (0 = event)\n\nFigure 9.2. What an event study looks for. Cumulative average residuals around an announcement at month 0. In an efficient market with a good-news announcement, the cumulative residual rises up to and at the event and then stays flat. Under-reaction produces a drift afterwards; over-reaction produces a reversal. Stylised illustration of the logic described on pp. 405–408, not data from the paper.\n\nStrong form (pp. 409–413)\nFama already says that the strong form is “probably best viewed as a benchmark” (p. 414). Two exceptions are documented: NYSE specialists who see unexecuted limit orders, and corporate insiders (p. 415). For everyone else the main test is on mutual funds (Jensen, 115 funds, 1955– 64). He compares each fund with a naive mix of the riskless asset and the market portfolio of the same risk (the “market line” of the Sharpe–Lintner model). Fama reports (pp. 412– 413): net of loading charges, 89 of 115 funds fell below the line and the average shortfall over ten years was 14.6%; ignoring loading charges, 72 of 115 and −8.9%; adding back all other expenses, 58 of 115 and −2.5%; adding estimated brokerage commissions, +0.09%, “which still is not indicative of the existence of special information.” Fama adds that the evidence is “not sufficiently precise to conclude that such information never exists” (footnote 32, p. 413)."}, {"pdf": 99, "printed": 72, "ch": 9, "text": "9.6 Fama’s conclusion and the one sentence you should not overquote\nOn p. 416 Fama writes that “the evidence in support of the efficient markets model is extensive, and (somewhat uniquely in economics) contradictory evidence is sparse,” and that the model is “a good first (and second) approximation to reality” for most investors. He then adds that “much remains to be done” and names the development and testing of models of market equilibrium under uncertainty as the most pressing task (p. 416). This is the joint-hypothesis problem again.\n\n9.7 Common misreadings\nFive things the paper did not say\n\n1. “Prices are always correct or rational.” The paper says only that prices fully reflect an information set, in a model of expected returns. It does not say they equal fundamental value in a deep sense.\n2. “Efficiency means a random walk.” The random walk is a special case (p. 387). Evidence against independence is not evidence against efficiency.\n3. “No one can ever earn above-average returns.” Expected returns above the\nequilibrium level are ruled out given the information set; earning more by taking more risk is not ruled out. And the strong form is explicitly a benchmark that fails for insiders and specialists.\n4. “Fama says the market is perfect.” He lists transaction costs, costly information and disagreement as real, potential sources of inefficiency (p. 388) and calls the hypothesis “an extreme null hypothesis” that no one expects to hold literally (p. 388).\n5. “Statistical significance disproves it.” He shows exactly why not: small correlations with large samples are significant but economically trivial (p. 394).\n\n9.8 Later debate (general knowledge, not in your file)\nCheck this section before citing. The paper’s reading of the evidence did not stay unchallenged. Studies of momentum, value and size, long-run reversals, and bubbles led many to doubt that prices fully reflect information (the field of behavioural finance, Shiller among others); Fama’s 1991 sequel renamed the tests (“return predictability”, “event studies”, “private information”) and stressed the joint-hypothesis problem. Grossman and Stiglitz (1980), which Stiglitz cites in your 2011 paper (p. 625), argued that perfect efficiency is impossible: if prices fully reflect information, nobody would pay to collect it. Chapter 10 uses that argument against securitisation.\n\n9.9 What it means for the thesis\nThis section is my inference about the thesis, not Fama’s claim."}, {"pdf": 100, "printed": 73, "ch": 9, "text": "An event-study design is available to you. If you want to see how bank equity responds to climate-related announcements (RBI climate circulars, disclosure mandates, or carbon-price news), the market model and cumulative abnormal return from this paper give you the method: estimate αj , βj in an estimation window, compute abnormal returns AR = r−(α̂+ β̂rM ) around the event, cumulate. The paper also tells you what to look for: a jump at the event and nothing after. But the efficient-market assumption is a choice, not a fact. If transition risk is Type 3 uncertainty (Phase 1, Knight and Keynes), prices may not “fully reflect” it in Fama’s sense, because there is no agreed expected-return model for it. A market-based measure of climate risk then reflects prices and beliefs, not objective risk. The thesis can say this plainly when it uses market-based measures such as CRISK in Phase 6. Caution about Indian public-sector bank stocks. They are partly state-owned and some trade thinly. Fama’s sufficient conditions (no costs, free information) are weakly met, and the paper itself says one should not assume efficiency by default. Test it, don’t assume it. Fat tails. The paper’s own finding that returns have fatter tails than the normal is a justification for using CVaR rather than variance in the thesis (Phase 5). Do you need the original? For the theory and conclusions: no. Open the original in these cases:\n• You will cite a specific empirical result. The numbers (Table 1, p. 393; split study, pp. 405–408; Jensen, pp. 412–413) are given above but check them if you quote them.\n• You will use the market model for an event study. Read pp. 402–408 for the exact method and the cumulative residual definition.\n• You want the Sharpe–Lintner equations (11)–(19). I have not derived them; see pp. 403–404 and 410–411.\n• You want the Treasury-bill work of Roll and the stable-distribution detail. See pp. 399– 402.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ9.1 State Fama’s definition of an efficient market and the three information sets that give the three forms.\nQ9.2 What is a fair game? A submartingale? A random walk? Which is the weakest?\nQ9.3 Name the three market conditions Fama says are sufficient for efficiency. Are they necessary?\nQ9.4 What did Jensen find about mutual funds? What are the two groups with documented strong-form advantages? Explain"}, {"pdf": 101, "printed": 74, "ch": 9, "text": "Q9.5 Why does Fama say that statistically significant serial correlation does not refute efficiency? Use the 0.06 example.\nQ9.6 Explain why the market model is needed to test the semi-strong form. What do the cumulative average residuals show if the market is efficient?\nQ9.7 Why does Fama say that a test of efficiency is always a joint test? What is the second hypothesis?\nQ9.8 Why can a market be efficient even if transaction costs are large (p. 388)? Apply\nQ9.9 A stock has α̂ = 0, β̂ = 1.2. On an announcement day the market returns +1.0% and the stock returns +2.5%. Compute the abnormal return. If the next four days have abnormal returns of +0.2%, −0.1%, +0.3%, 0.0%, what is the cumulative abnormal return over the five days?\nQ9.10 You find that bank stock returns on RBI climate-circular days are not different from zero. List two reasons other than “banks are unaffected by climate risk”.\nQ9.11 Suppose your estimated daily serial correlation for an index is 0.04 with N = 2,500. Compute the approximate standard error and say whether it is significant and economically meaningful. Viva\nQ9.12 “Fama’s efficient markets contradict Knight and Keynes: markets cannot be efficient under Knightian uncertainty.” Respond in four sentences.\nQ9.13 “Your thesis uses CRISK, which is based on equity returns. Why should equity returns reveal anything about climate risk if markets do not price it?” How do you answer?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA9.1 Prices at every time “fully reflect” available information. Weak: past prices; semi-strong: all public information; strong: all information including private.\nA9.2 Fair game: surprises have zero conditional mean given the information. Submartingale: expected next price ≥ current price. Random walk: successive returns independent and identically distributed. The fair game (expected return) model is the weakest.\nA9.3 No transaction costs; all information free to all; all agree on its implications. No, they are sufficient not necessary (p. 388).\nA9.4 Funds in aggregate do not beat the market line after expenses (89 of 115 below the line net of loads); no consistent individual winners. Documented strong-form advantages: NYSE specialists and corporate insiders.\nA9.5 With thousands of observations small correlations are many standard errors from zero, but 0.062 = 0.0036: it explains 0.36% of variation, too little to cover costs. Significance and economic importance are different.\nA9.6 The market model gives the normal relationship between a stock and the market, so"}, {"pdf": 102, "printed": 75, "ch": 9, "text": "that residuals measure the part due to the event. If efficient, Um jumps at the event and is flat afterwards.\nA9.7 Because the claim “prices fully reflect Φt ” only has content after specifying the equilibrium expected-return model (CAPM, etc.). If the test fails you cannot tell which part is wrong.\nA9.8 Because efficiency requires only that when transactions occur the price reflects the information; costs reduce the number of trades, not the information content of prices (unless they block transactions altogether).\nA9.9 AR = 2.5 − (0 + 1.2 × 1.0) = 1.3%. CAR = 1.3 + 0.2 − 0.1 + 0.3 + 0.0 = 1.7%.\nA9.10 (i) Announcement was anticipated (Waud found anticipation); (ii) the window contaminated by other news; (iii) thin trading and noise, or low power; (iv) the circular is not new information. Any two.\nA9.11 SE ≈ 1/ 2500 = 0.02. 0.04 is two standard errors, so borderline significant; but\n0.042 = 0.16% of variance is economically negligible.\nA9.12 Fama’s model presupposes a known return distribution given the information, i.e., risk, not Knightian uncertainty. Under Type 3 uncertainty there is no accepted equilibrium model, so a test is not possible or not informative. This is a limit of the efficient-market test, not a refutation of Knight. A defensible position is to use markets as a source of information about beliefs, not as proof of correct pricing.\nA9.13 Equity returns reveal market beliefs and bank exposure to systemic shocks, even if they do not price climate risk correctly. CRISK is conditional on a stated market decline, not a claim that prices are right. If prices underreact, CRISK is a lower bound on a belief-based measure."}, {"pdf": 103, "printed": 76, "ch": 10, "text": "CHAPTER 10\n\nStiglitz (2011): Rethinking Macroeconomics: What Failed, and How to Repair It\nSource line\nJoseph E. Stiglitz, “Rethinking Macroeconomics: What Failed, and How to Repair It”, Journal of the European Economic Association 9(4), August 2011, pp. 591–645 (the Adam Smith Lecture, delivered in Glasgow, 24 August 2010). Page numbers below are the printed journal pages. This is a lecture-essay: it argues, illustrates and recommends. It does not estimate a model, so what you can cite from it is an argument and a list of mechanisms, not a coefficient.\n\n10.1 What the paper is and why it matters for you\nAfter the 2007–09 crisis, a large part of the economics profession had to explain why its main workhorse, the dynamic stochastic general equilibrium (DSGE) model, did not predict it and gave little guidance during it. Stiglitz calls the workhorse the “Standard Model” or “Conventional Wisdom” and argues that the failure was not one of economic management (the view he attributes to Bernanke) but of the models (pp. 591–593). For your thesis this paper is the bridge between three things you have already met:\n• the credit-rationing and moral-hazard results of Stiglitz–Weiss (Chapter 7) and Tirole (Chapter 8), now applied to the whole economy;\n• the efficient-market claim of Fama (Chapter 9), which Stiglitz attacks directly (p. 625);\n• the financial instability view of Keynes and Minsky (Phase 1), which Lavoie develops (Chapter 12). It also contains, at pp. 614–615 and p. 619, two short passages about bank capital-adequacy constraints that bear directly on a thesis about capital adequacy. Where Stiglitz sits\n\nLavoie (2014, pp. 9–10, 71) classes Stiglitz as an orthodox dissenter: someone who criticises the mainstream sharply but works inside its methods (optimising agents, equilibrium, imperfect information as the “friction”). Lavoie quotes the remark that Stiglitz’s mission was “to undermine free market fundamentalism from within”. Post-Keynesians (Chapters 11–12) share many of his conclusions but not his method. This distinction matters for how you position the thesis (Chapter 13)."}, {"pdf": 104, "printed": 77, "ch": 10, "text": "10.2 The target: the Standard Model\nStiglitz’s complaint has a precise structure. The Standard Model is built so that, in its core form, the following are absent: information asymmetries, a financial sector with real balance sheets, debt, bankruptcy, and heterogeneity among agents (p. 598). Because there is one representative household and one representative firm, there is no one to lend to and borrow from. Shocks come from outside the model, the government is outside the model, and “money” stands in for credit. Table 10.1. Stiglitz’s main criticisms of the Standard Model (section 2, pp. 594–607). The page numbers are those of the discussion, not of every mention. Feature\n\nWhy Stiglitz objects\n\nPages\n\nModels as blinders\n\nA model decides what the economist can see; a model with no 594–597\nbankruptcies cannot ask about bankruptcies.\nRepresentative agent Removes lenders and borrowers, hence debt, deleveraging and de- 598–600 fault; distribution is irrelevant by construction. “As-if” modelling Agents are assumed to behave as if they solved the model; the as- 598–601 sumption is not tested against how people decide. Rational expecta- Everyone knows the true model; in a world of asymmetric informa- 602–603 tions tion and disagreement this is not neutral. Grossman–Stiglitz is cited in a footnote. Exogenous shocks The crisis arose inside the financial sector; treating shocks as exter- 601–605 and government nal hides the mechanism. Money, not credit What matters is credit availability and bank capital, not the money 603–605 supply. Markets not fully ra- Quotes Greenspan’s “shocked disbelief” that markets did not self- 599–600 tional correct (p. 600). Ad hoc “wedges” Patching a model by adding frictions (“wedges”) after the fact is not 606–607 an explanation.\n\nFootnote 7 makes a point worth remembering for method: models are often chosen because they have a unique steady state that is ergodic (the system returns to it whatever happens), which is mathematically convenient. The convenience, not the realism, drives the choice. This ties directly to Phase 1: ergodicity is the technical form of “the future resembles the past”, which Knight and Keynes deny.\n\n10.3 Section 3: why was the downturn so deep and so long?\n(pp. 607–620)\nStiglitz poses three puzzles: the origin of the crisis (why a bubble), the amplification (why a housing-sector loss became a global downturn), and the persistence (why recovery was slow).\n\nOrigin: bubbles are endogenous (p. 610)\nBubbles are not exogenous accidents; they are fed by the same system that later breaks. Lending against collateral raises asset prices, which raises collateral values, which allows more lending. Stiglitz stresses that in this setting the usual defence of the market (“prices are set by"}, {"pdf": 105, "printed": 78, "ch": 10, "text": "informed traders”) does not work, because the traders making the market have a stake in the bubble.\n\nAmplification: a list of mechanisms (pp. 611–617)\nStiglitz’s list is long. Table 10.2 groups the entries into the mechanisms that matter for a bank-centred thesis. The page references are those of the printed paper. Table 10.2. Amplification and persistence mechanisms in Stiglitz (2011), section 3. Page ranges are approximate section ranges (exact only for p. 617 and p. 619 quotations); check before quoting. Mechanism\n\nHow it works\n\nPages\n\nFinancial constraints, A fall in asset prices cuts borrowers’ net worth, which tightens 613–614 financial accelerator credit, which lowers investment and demand, which lowers asset prices further. Bank lending Bank net worth falls with losses. Capital-adequacy constraints then 614–615 reduce lending “by a multiple” of the loss. Bankruptcy cascades One default cuts the net worth of its creditors, who may then fail; 615–616 and contagion networks make this worse. Trend reinforcement, Past price changes shape expected ones (reference to Battiston et 611–613 expectations al. 2010); amplifies booms and busts. Non-linearities Small shocks absorbed; large ones cross thresholds (default, margin 613 calls). New uncertainties After the shock, no one knows who holds what; lending freezes. 616–617 Persistence Slow balance-sheet re- Households and firms must rebuild net worth before borrowing 617–618 pair again. Debt deflation (Fisher) Falling prices raise the real value of debt (p. 617). 617 Collateral shrinkage Lower prices reduce the borrowing capacity of all collateral-based 617–619 borrowers. Capital-adequacy rules Stiglitz: “rigid capital adequacy standards for banks can be 619 destabilizing; countercyclical capital adequacy standards (macroprudential regulation) can be stabilizing” (p. 619). Who bears the loss Delay while creditors, debtors and the state fight over losses; a 619–620 “Super-Chapter 11” (fast restructuring) is proposed. In plain words: why capital rules can destabilise\n\nSuppose a bank must hold capital of at least 8% of its risky assets. Then each rupee of capital supports about Rs 12.50 of lending. If a shock destroys Rs 10 of capital, lending must fall by about Rs 125 unless the bank can raise new capital. In a crisis it cannot. So the same rule that makes banks safe in good times forces them to shrink in bad times, which lowers asset prices and capital again. A rule that tightens in booms and relaxes in busts (countercyclical) breaks the loop. (The 8% and the arithmetic are my illustration, not Stiglitz’s numbers.)"}, {"pdf": 106, "printed": 79, "ch": 10, "text": "Asset prices\nand collateral fall\n\nBank net worth\n(capital) falls\n\nfeedback loop\n\nDefaults rise;\nasset sales\n\nBorrowers' net worth\nand profits fall\n\nCapital rule binds:\nlending cut by a\nmultiple\n\nmultiplier k\n\nInvestment and\ndemand fall\n\nA countercyclical capital rule weakens the 'binds' step and cuts the loop (Stiglitz 2011, p. 619).\n\nFigure 10.1. The bank-balance-sheet feedback loop in Stiglitz (2011), pp. 613–619. Each arrow is a mechanism from Table 10.2. The circled multiplier is the lending multiple on bank capital (compare k in Tirole, Chapter 8). A countercyclical capital rule cuts the loop at the multiplier.\n\n10.4 Section 4: four hypotheses about the crisis (pp. 620–630)\n1. Risk (4.1, pp. 621–624). Risk was mispriced and misallocated: instruments meant to spread risk concentrated it where it could not be absorbed.\n2. Information (4.2, pp. 624–626). Securitisation and derivatives made it impossible to know who held what; transparency fell. Here Stiglitz appeals to Grossman–Stiglitz (1980) against the efficient-markets view (p. 625): if prices reflected all information, no one would pay to acquire it, so markets cannot be fully informationally efficient.\n3. Credit markets (4.3, pp. 626–627). Shadow banking, higher leverage under Basel II, reliance on wholesale deposits; once losses came, banks had too little capital.\n4. Structural transformation (4.4, pp. 628–629). Movement of labour out of manufacturing and growing inequality left aggregate demand weak, propped up by debt. His conclusion is that the models must put information, credit and distribution at the centre, not add them as afterthoughts.\n\n10.5 Sections 5 and 6: policy and concluding remarks (pp. 630–636)\nPolicy. Fiscal multipliers are not constant: they differ with the state of the financial system and the type of spending or tax. Bank net worth matters for monetary transmission. At the zero lower bound conventional rate cuts lose force. Monetary policy is itself endogenous to the model rather than a clean exogenous lever. Concluding remarks. Stiglitz asks the profession to ask different questions, not just to refine answers to old ones. He criticises verification by moment matching (calibrate until the model reproduces a few statistics; Korinek is cited) as a weak test, stresses non-convexities, and calls for a “New Macroeconomics” built around the elements above."}, {"pdf": 107, "printed": 80, "ch": 10, "text": "10.6 Common misreadings\nFive things the paper did not say\n\n1. “Economic theory is useless.” Stiglitz wants better theory, not less (section 6). His complaint is about which assumptions are treated as innocent.\n2. “Markets are always irrational.” The claim is narrower: the assumptions of full rationality and full information are not adequate for understanding a financial crisis.\n3. “Capital requirements are bad.” The statement on p. 619 is about rigid, procyclical standards. He argues for countercyclical ones.\n4. “He has a finished alternative model.” He does not. The paper lists the elements an alternative should have; it does not present one.\n5. “He agrees with post-Keynesians.” Many conclusions overlap, but his method is optimisation under imperfect information (see Lavoie’s classification above).\n\n10.7 What it means for the thesis\nThis section is my inference about the thesis, not Stiglitz’s claim.\nA transition shock as an amplified shock. The mechanisms in Table 10.2 give you a vocabulary for how a climate-transition shock (repricing of carbon-intensive assets) could move from borrowers’ balance sheets to bank capital. Fall in asset value → lower borrower net worth and collateral → higher credit risk and lower lending capacity → lower investment, including green investment → further stress. If a bank is near its capital floor, the shock is amplified by the multiplier. Capital adequacy as a transmission channel. Your thesis question (does climate transition risk affect capital adequacy of Indian public-sector banks) is exactly the channel Stiglitz describes at pp. 614–615. His point is that the ratio is not only an outcome but also a constraint that shapes lending. This is an argument for testing both directions: transition risk → capital ratio, and capital ratio → lending to transition-exposed sectors. Counter-cyclical rules and climate. The p. 619 argument is a ready-made frame for discussing whether Basel capital buffers should be climate-sensitive. Disclosure. The information argument (pp. 624–626) supports the case for mandatory climate disclosure (in India, the BRSR framework): if no one knows who holds climate-exposed assets, the freeze mechanism applies. I do not know the exact scope of BRSR for your sample; check before you write about it. Limits to respect. (i) The paper is an argument, not an estimated model, so you cannot cite it for a size of effect. (ii) It is about a developed-country, market-based financial crisis; Indian public-sector banks are state-owned, lend mainly through branches and have implicit sovereign backing, which changes how cascades and capital raising work. (iii) The recommendation to abandon rational expectations supports the use of scenario analysis (Phase 6) over forecasts."}, {"pdf": 108, "printed": 81, "ch": 10, "text": "Do you need the original?\nFor the argument and the mechanisms: no.\nOpen the original in these cases:\n• You will quote it. Use exact wording from pp. 598, 614–615, 619 and 625, not my paraphrase.\n• You want the bank-lending mechanism in his own words (pp. 614–615). It is short; read it.\n• You want the discussion of the zero lower bound, fiscal multipliers or specific policy proposals (section 5). I summarised only.\n• You want the references to Greenwald–Stiglitz and other formal models he cites. I did not reproduce them.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ10.1 What does Stiglitz mean by the “Standard Model”? Name four things absent from it.\nQ10.2 List the three puzzles of section 3 and one mechanism for each.\nQ10.3 What are the four hypotheses of section 4?\nQ10.4 What does Stiglitz say about rigid versus countercyclical capital adequacy standards (p. 619)? Explain\nQ10.5 Explain the financial accelerator in a loop of five steps, using a bank rather than a firm as the borrower.\nQ10.6 Why does the representative-agent assumption make bankruptcy impossible to model?\nQ10.7 What is Grossman–Stiglitz’s point about information, and why does it contradict the strong form of Fama’s hypothesis?\nQ10.8 Why does Lavoie call Stiglitz an orthodox dissenter and not a heterodox economist? Apply\nQ10.9 A bank has capital Rs 100 and risk-weighted assets Rs 1,000 (ratio 10%), minimum 9%. A shock wipes out Rs 20 of capital. If it cannot raise capital, by how much must it shrink risk-weighted assets to restore 9%? Compare this with a case where the minimum is lowered to 8% in the downturn.\nQ10.10 Pick one Indian public-sector bank. List which items from Table 10.2 apply to it in a transition-risk scenario and which do not (for example, wholesale funding).\nQ10.11 Suppose a regulator requires banks to build extra climate-related provisions during good years and lets them draw those down in a stress year. Use the paper to explain the likely effect, and contrast it with a rule that demands extra provisions only after losses appear."}, {"pdf": 109, "printed": 82, "ch": 10, "text": "Viva\nQ10.12 “Stiglitz offers no model, so his paper cannot support a thesis.” Respond.\nQ10.13 “If markets were as irrational as Stiglitz says, central banks and regulators (also human) would be just as bad.” How might Stiglitz and a post-Keynesian answer differently?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA10.1 The DSGE-based consensus model. Absent: information asymmetries, a real financial sector, debt and bankruptcy, heterogeneity (also credit as opposed to money, endogenous shocks).\nA10.2 Origin: endogenous bubbles fed by collateral lending. Amplification: financial accelerator or bank capital constraints. Persistence: slow balance-sheet repair or Fisher debt deflation.\nA10.3 Risk, information, credit markets, structural transformation.\nA10.4 Rigid standards can be destabilising; countercyclical ones (macroprudential regulation) can be stabilising.\nA10.5 Asset prices fall; bank capital falls; capital rule forces lending to fall (by a multiple); investment and demand fall; asset prices fall further. The bank takes the place of the firm because the constraint applies to its net worth.\nA10.6 With one agent there is no one to owe money to; a loan is just a transfer from the agent to itself, so default has no meaning.\nA10.7 If prices fully reflected all information, nobody would pay to gather information, so nobody would make prices informative; hence perfect efficiency is impossible. The strong form requires prices to reflect even private information.\nA10.8 Because his critique keeps optimisation, equilibrium and information-based frictions; Lavoie calls the heterodox position one that rejects the core methodology (Chapter 11).\nA10.9 Required assets = 100 − 20 = 80 of capital /0.09 = Rs 888.9; shrink by about Rs 111 (from 1,000). With 8%: 80/0.08 = 1,000, so no shrinkage. The countercyclical relaxation removes the forced cut.\nA10.10 Answers differ by bank; a sound answer separates channels that apply (collateral values, borrower net worth, capital ratio) from those that do not (securitisation, shadow banking, wholesale funding, which is small for public-sector banks).\nA10.11 Building buffers in good years and releasing them in stress is the countercyclical direction Stiglitz favours (p. 619): it avoids forced shrinkage when capital is scarce. A rule that demands extra provisions only after losses appear is procyclical and would deepen the loop in Figure 10.1.\nA10.12 The paper offers mechanisms and a list of ingredients, which suffices for framing a research question and choosing variables; the estimation comes from elsewhere (Phases 4 to 6). A thesis needs a theoretical framing, not a single model.\nA10.13 Stiglitz: policy-makers face different incentives and information; institutions and rules"}, {"pdf": 110, "printed": 83, "ch": 10, "text": "(countercyclical standards, resolution regimes) can correct errors. Post-Keynesian: fundamental uncertainty means no one can compute the right answer, so rules should be simple and robust, and the state acts as lender and spender of last resort."}, {"pdf": 111, "printed": 84, "ch": 11, "text": "CHAPTER 11\n\nLavoie (2014), Chapters 1 and 2: What\nIs Post-Keynesian Economics?\nSource line\nMarc Lavoie, Post-Keynesian Economics: New Foundations (Edward Elgar, 2014): Chapter 1, “Essentials of heterodox and post-Keynesian economics” (pp. 1–71 in the printed book) and Chapter 2, “Theory of choice” (the pages I cite are 83–115). Your files are scanned chapters; I read them by OCR, so a few words may be garbled. Printed page = PDF page −15 in your files. Lavoie is a textbook-style survey. He reports the field’s positions, with his own judgments marked in the first person; where I say “Lavoie argues” it is his view, not necessarily the whole school’s.\n\n11.1 Why this chapter is in the reading list\nPhases 1 and 2 so far gave you the orthodox side (Fama, and Stiglitz and Weiss as an orthodoxwith-frictions extension) and the foundations of uncertainty (Knight, Keynes). Lavoie supplies the map that connects them: what the post-Keynesian school believes, how it differs from the orthodox and from orthodox dissenters like Stiglitz, and what theory of choice follows from taking uncertainty seriously. One-paragraph summary\n\nPost-Keynesians hold that the economy is demand-led even in the long run, that investment determines saving, that money and credit are central, that time is historical (irreversible), and that the future is fundamentally uncertain. From the last point follow a different theory of choice (people use rules and habits, not optimisation) and a different theory of finance (stability breeds fragility). Chapter 12 takes the financial half further.\n\n11.2 Chapter 1: orthodox, heterodox, dissenter (pp. 8–10, 34–38)\nLavoie begins with a classification that matters for how you position the thesis. Following Backhouse (2004), he separates the mainstream (the textbook consensus) from dissenters, and he divides the dissenters into two groups:\n• Orthodox dissenters work with mainstream methods and criticise from inside. Lavoie’s examples include Shiller, Thaler, Akerlof, Krugman, Stiglitz and Williamson (p. 10). He quotes the remark that Stiglitz’s mission was “to undermine free market fundamentalism from within” (p. 71). Lee’s more provocative word is heretics."}, {"pdf": 112, "printed": 85, "ch": 11, "text": "• Heterodox dissenters reject the core of the mainstream method: post-Keynesians, Marxians, radicals, old institutionalists. Lee calls them blasphemers. An orthodox dissenter can become mainstream (Lavoie’s examples: Friedman in the 1950s; the New Consensus/DSGE model, which began as dissent). A heterodox dissenter is unlikely to. This explains why Stiglitz (Chapter 10) and Lavoie can reach similar conclusions on financial instability yet still disagree on method. A caution on ideology (p. 26)\n\nLavoie notes that neoclassical theory can be extended with auxiliary hypotheses (externalities, asymmetric information, increasing returns) to justify almost any policy, and cites Stiglitz as having said that this was his academic strategy. He concludes that the link between a person’s politics and their school of thought “may not be overly tight”.\n\nThe post-Keynesian presuppositions (pp. 34–37)\nTable 11.1. Post-Keynesian presuppositions as Lavoie lists them (Table 1.6; pp. 34–37). The right column is the orthodox contrast I have inferred from his text. Presupposition\n\nWhat post-Keynesians hold\n\nOrthodox contrast (my wording)\n\nEffective demand\n\nOutput and employment are demand- Demand matters in the short run; longdetermined in the short and the long run growth is supply-driven. run; the actual path affects long-run supply. Investment deter- Investment is autonomous of house- Saving decisions determine investment. mines saving holds’ intertemporal choice. Institutions matter Fiscal and monetary policy affect real Policy is neutral in the long run. quantities in the long run. Monetized economy Contracts, debts and assets are in Money is a unit of account; real analymoney; money is not a veil. sis comes first. Historical time Irreversible; the traverse affects the Logical time; equilibrium independent destination (path dependence, hystere- of path. sis). Fundamental uncer- “Only the past is known, the future is Risk: all outcomes and probabilities tainty uncertain” (Eichner and Kregel). known. Non-ergodicity Time averages and space averages dif- Ergodic: the future is a draw from the fer, so past averages cannot guide the same distribution. future (Davidson). Specific micro, power, Less agreed; Lavoie doubts that plural- — distribution, open sys- ism and open systems are specific to the school. tems, pluralism\n\nLavoie ties the last two rows directly to finance: non-ergodicity is “associated with black swans and fat tails, as well as large switches in expectations and confidence, leaving little room for the empirical worthiness of rational expectations and the efficient-market hypothesis” (p. 36). That is the sentence which links this chapter to Fama (Chapter 9) and to Knight and Keynes (Phase 1)."}, {"pdf": 113, "printed": 86, "ch": 11, "text": "Econometrics (p. 37)\nBecause Phase 4 of your reading plan (Wooldridge, Blundell–Bond, Roodman) is econometric, Lavoie’s short discussion matters. He reports two attitudes: the Fundamentalist strand (following Lawson and Keynes’s “statistical alchemy”) doubts that economic data has enough event regularities for econometrics; but “in general, post-Keynesians have embraced econometric methods, especially since 2000” (p. 37), with Kalecki as the early example. He gives pragmatic reasons: policy-makers need numbers, econometrics clarifies debate, and it is “a potent rhetorical tool”. He also notes methods congenial to the school: the Hendry/LSE general-to-specific approach, and cointegrated VARs (Juselius), because non-stationary data fit the idea that equilibrium depends on the path. He is also frank that few results are robust or replicable.\n\nThe strands (Table 1.7, p. 38)\nFive strands: Fundamentalists (Keynes’s School; Davidson, Minsky, Chick, Moore; financial instability, liquidity preference), Kaleckians, Sraffians, Institutionalists, Kaldorians. Minsky belongs to the first. The Kaleckian strand is the one behind most formal macro models, including the stock-flow-consistent ones of Phase 6.\n\n11.3 Chapter 2: theory of choice (pp. 83–115)\nFour kinds of rationality (Table 2.2, p. 83)\nLavoie borrows Gigerenzer’s taxonomy:\nTable 11.2. Four kinds of rationality (Table 2.2, p. 83, shortened).\nKind\n\nIdea\n\nWho (Lavoie’s list)\n\n(a) Unbounded\n\nAgents know everything relevant and optimise; Rational expectations, general\n“as if” theory.\nequilibrium, efficient markets,\nDSGE; Lucas, Arrow, Woodford.\n(b) Bounded optimi- Optimise subject to the cost of search or infor- Search theory, Stigler, Sarsation mation. gent. (c) Cognitive illusions People deviate systematically from (a): biases. Kahneman, Thaler, Camerer, Akerlof, Shiller (the new behavioural economics). (d) Environment- People use rules, habits and heuristics suited to Keynes, Gigerenzer, Simon and Carnegie School, Katona, consistent (ecologi- their environment. Nelson–Winter. cal)\n\nLavoie’s argument is that (c), the new behavioural economics, is a form of orthodox dissent: it keeps (a) as the benchmark and calls the departures errors. Post-Keynesians prefer (d) and the old behavioural economics, which treats rules as reasonable responses to a world where optimising is impossible. This links to Fama: efficient markets sit in cell (a)."}, {"pdf": 114, "printed": 87, "ch": 11, "text": "Why optimisation fails: two kinds of uncertainty\n\nLavoie distinguishes substantive uncertainty (an information gap: you lack data) from procedural uncertainty (a competence gap: you have the data but cannot compute the best answer; Heiner’s phrase). Even with all information, the problem may be too complex to solve. In that case the rational response is a rule, and Heiner argues that this is why behaviour shows regularities: rules are what produce predictable behaviour under uncertainty (pp. 94–95).\n\nFast and frugal heuristics (p. 88–90)\nLavoie reports Gigerenzer’s finding that simple rules can predict better out of sample than optimising ones. In the dropout-rate example, a multiple regression fits the first half of the schools best but predicts the second half worse than “take the best” (one cue) or equal weights (Figure 2.1, p. 89). His lesson: “complex strategies, like multiple regressions, work well in predicting the past; they are no good at predicting the future” (p. 89). He applies the same logic to DSGE models, which fitted the past and failed in 2008 (pp. 89–90), and to prospect theory, which has more free parameters than expected utility. Do not over-read this\n\nThe claim concerns overfitting. It is not a case against regression. A properly crossvalidated regression is not the target. For your thesis this is a reason to report out-ofsample or robustness checks (Phase 4), not to avoid econometrics.\n\nConsumer choice principles (pp. 98–115)\nLavoie lists principles of the post-Keynesian theory of consumer choice: procedural rationality (rules, habits), satiable needs, separability of needs, subordination of needs (a lexicographic ordering in which higher needs are met only after lower ones), and Lancaster’s characteristics approach (goods valued for their properties). I did not extract the detailed discussion of these; the chapter’s Sections after p. 98 develop them, and for a thesis on banks they are the least relevant part. Skip unless you teach microeconomics.\n\n11.4 Common misreadings\nFour things Lavoie does not say\n\n1. “Post-Keynesians reject mathematics and econometrics.” He says the opposite for most of the school (p. 37), apart from the Fundamentalist strand.\n2. “Post-Keynesian and New Keynesian are the same.” New Keynesians such as\nStiglitz are orthodox dissenters; the method differs.\n3. “Behavioural economics is post-Keynesian.” Only the old behavioural economics (Simon, Katona) fits; the new one is orthodox dissent (Table 11.2).\n4. “Uncertainty means anything goes.” Heiner’s point: uncertainty produces regularities, through rules."}, {"pdf": 115, "printed": 88, "ch": 11, "text": "11.5 What it means for the thesis\nThis section is my inference about the thesis, not Lavoie’s claim.\nPositioning. A thesis on climate transition risk and capital adequacy can sit in the orthodoxdissent space (information frictions, Stiglitz–Weiss, Tirole) and can still borrow the postKeynesian view that climate-transition outcomes are fundamentally uncertain. This is the eclecticism the Phase 1 “levels” table allowed: use risk-based tools where a distribution can be estimated, and flag where you rely on judgement. Be careful to say which assumptions come from which school rather than blending them silently. Methods. Lavoie’s discussion of econometrics permits you to use panel econometrics while stating that parameters need not be stable (non-ergodicity). Report robustness. Out-of-sample caution. The overfitting argument is a good reason to test your climate-risk models outside the sample, and to be modest about forecasts. Do you need the original? For the map of the school and the rationality taxonomy: no. Open the original in these cases:\n• You will quote a presupposition or a classification. Use pp. 34–38 and 83 directly.\n• You want the full strands description. I gave the first strand only; read pp. 38–45.\n• You want the consumer-choice theory. I skipped pp. 98–115 detail.\n• You want Chapter 6 (growth and financial fragility models). I have not covered it: your plan lists Lavoie Chapters 1, 2 and 6, but your folder holds Chapters 1, 2 and\n4. Chapter 6 is where Lavoie formalises Minskyan fragility. Send it if you want it added.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ11.1 Distinguish mainstream, orthodox dissenters and heterodox dissenters. Where does Lavoie put Stiglitz?\nQ11.2 List four post-Keynesian presuppositions that Lavoie calls specific to the school.\nQ11.3 Name Gigerenzer’s four kinds of rationality and place Fama’s efficient-market hypothesis.\nQ11.4 What is the difference between substantive and procedural uncertainty?\nExplain\nQ11.5 Why does non-ergodicity leave “little room” for rational expectations?\nQ11.6 Why is the “investment determines saving” claim linked to the principle of effective demand?\nQ11.7 Why does Lavoie say that the new behavioural economics is orthodox dissent?"}, {"pdf": 116, "printed": 89, "ch": 11, "text": "Q11.8 Explain, using the dropout-rate example, why a model that fits the past best may predict worst. Apply\nQ11.9 Classify these as orthodox, orthodox-dissent or heterodox-dissent: (i) a DSGE model with a financial accelerator; (ii) a stock-flow-consistent model with endogenous money; (iii) Stiglitz–Weiss; (iv) a CAPM event study.\nQ11.10 Your panel regression on bank capital fits well in-sample. Give two tests that would reveal overfitting, in the spirit of Gigerenzer.\nQ11.11 Give an example from Indian banking of a rule or habit that works under procedural uncertainty, and say what a bounded-optimisation model would add. Viva\nQ11.12 “Post-Keynesian theory is not testable because uncertainty is unmeasurable.” Reply, using Lavoie on econometrics and Heiner on regularities.\nQ11.13 “You use both Stiglitz–Weiss and post-Keynesian ideas. Isn’t that inconsistent?” Answer in five sentences.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA11.1 Mainstream: textbook consensus. Orthodox dissenters: critical but use mainstream methods. Heterodox dissenters: reject the core method. Stiglitz: orthodox dissenter (p. 10, p. 71).\nA11.2 Any four of: effective demand in the long run; investment determines saving; institutions matter; monetized economy; historical time; fundamental uncertainty; non-ergodicity.\nA11.3 Unbounded (a), bounded optimisation (b), cognitive illusions (c), environmentconsistent/ecological (d). Efficient markets: (a).\nA11.4 Substantive: lack of information. Procedural: inability to process it (competence gap).\nA11.5 If past averages do not predict the future, agents cannot know the true distribution, which rational expectations requires.\nA11.6 Investment is decided by expected profit, not by households’ saving; income adjusts so that saving equals investment, which is effective demand at work.\nA11.7 It keeps unbounded rationality as the benchmark and treats deviations as errors rather than rational responses to uncertainty.\nA11.8 Regression exploits every detail of the first half, including noise; on new data the noise is different, so accuracy falls. Simple rules ignore noise.\nA11.9 (i) orthodox dissent; (ii) heterodox; (iii) orthodox dissent; (iv) orthodox (mainstream finance).\nA11.10 Out-of-sample prediction on a hold-out period or bank group; leave-one-bank-out crossvalidation; stability across sub-samples; fewer regressors."}, {"pdf": 117, "printed": 90, "ch": 11, "text": "A11.11 E.g. a rule of lending only against collateral of at least some multiple, or a standard haircut. A bounded-optimisation model would add the cost of gathering information about each borrower.\nA11.12 Lavoie: post-Keynesians use econometrics, with caution (p. 37); Heiner: uncertainty creates rule-based regularities that can be studied. Unmeasurable is not unobservable.\nA11.13 Not necessarily: they work at different levels. Stiglitz–Weiss explains a specific market mechanism (rationing) and is testable; the post-Keynesian view frames the macro uncertainty. The thesis should state which claims it takes from which, and which would survive if the other were dropped. Lavoie himself says orthodox dissenters and postKeynesians reach similar policy conclusions by different routes."}, {"pdf": 118, "printed": 91, "ch": 12, "text": "CHAPTER 12\n\nLavoie (2014), Chapter 4: Credit, Money\nand Central Banks\nSource line\nMarc Lavoie, Post-Keynesian Economics: New Foundations (Edward Elgar, 2014), Chapter 4, “Credit, money and central banks” (starting at printed p. 182; your file is an OCR of the chapter). Printed page = PDF page −15. I read sections 4.1–4.2, 4.3.1, 4.3.4 and 4.5.4–4.5.5 closely. I did not read in detail sections 4.3.2–4.3.3 and 4.3.5–4.3.14 (bank profits, clearing, central-bank operations, chartalism), 4.4 (new developments in monetary policy: corridors, floors, quantitative easing), 4.5.1–4.5.3 (controversies over liquidity preference), 4.5.6 (shadow banking) and 4.6 (stock-flow-consistent accounting). Where I mention them it is only from the headings.\n\n12.1 Why this chapter matters for a thesis on capital adequacy\nAlmost every claim in a bank capital-adequacy study rests on a view of what a bank is. Is it a pipe that passes savers’ deposits to borrowers (the mainstream picture), or a creator of credit limited by its own balance sheet and by creditworthy demand (the post-Keynesian picture)? If banks are pipes, capital is a buffer; if they create credit, capital is the main brake on credit. Lavoie argues the second view, with an explicit discussion of capital-adequacy ratios in section 4.3.4 and credit rationing in section 4.5.4.\n\n12.2 The main claims: post-Keynesian versus mainstream (section\n4.2, pp. 187–190)\nThe last row is the one to remember. It places Chapter 7 (Stiglitz–Weiss) and this chapter on opposite sides of an explicit debate, and Lavoie says so directly (p. 189, p. 250): “When credit markets freeze, it is not because there is a sudden rise in asymmetric information; rather it is because the degree of uncertainty has been rising, generating a loss of confidence and an increase in the liquidity preference of bankers” (p. 250). Two more points from section 4.1:\n• Endogenous money is no longer unique to the school: New Consensus (DSGE) models also assume a central bank that sets the rate while the money supply adjusts. But in those models money plays no role beyond a unit of account (pp. 183–184).\n• Inside post-Keynesianism there was a long horizontalist–structuralist dispute: horizontalists (Kaldor, Moore, Godley) stressed that the supply of credit is elastic at the"}, {"pdf": 119, "printed": 92, "ch": 12, "text": "Table 12.1. Money and credit: post-Keynesian versus mainstream (Lavoie’s Table 4.1, p. 187; wording shortened). Feature\n\nPost-Keynesian\n\nSupply of money\nWhere money comes from\nMain concern\nMoney is tied to\nCausality\nBanks are\nReserve mechanism (if any)\n\nendogenous, demand-led\nexogenous\ncounterpart entries (loans)\n“helicopter”\ndebts and credits\nassets, money\nproduction, social relations\nprivate exchange\ncredits make deposits\ndeposits allow credits\ncreators of credit flows\nfinancial intermediaries\ndivisor (reserves a fraction of de- multiplier\nposits)\nlack of confidence\nasymmetric information\n\nCredit rationing is due to\n\nMainstream\n\nrate the central bank sets; structuralists (Chick, Dow, Palley, Wray, Pollin) stressed that spreads, liquidity preference and confidence shift. Lavoie considers that the debate has largely petered out (p. 184).\n\n12.3 A pure credit economy with one bank (4.3.1, pp. 193–196)\nLavoie builds the story from the simplest case: one bank, no cash, no reserves, no government, no defaults. Everyone has an account that is either in debit (a loan B) or in credit (a deposit D). With no leaks, B = D. Where do new loans come from? In the mainstream story, new deposits first. Lavoie’s answer: “Loans are created ex nihilo, at the stroke of a pen, or by punching a key on the computer, as long as the borrower is creditworthy, that is, as long as the borrower can show some collateral” (p. 193). The limit is the volume of loans that can be granted to creditworthy borrowers. In plain words: the double entry\n\nSuppose a firm borrows Rs 100. The bank writes “loan to firm: +100” on the asset side and “firm deposit: +100” on the liability side. No one saved anything first. The deposit exists because the loan does. When the firm pays wages, the deposit moves to households. When the firm repays the loan, both entries vanish. This is the reflux principle: money created by lending returns to the bank as loans are repaid. The mechanics are developed in the T-account tables 4.4–4.6 (overdraft facilities, firms’ use of credit lines). I have not reproduced them.\n\n12.4 Bank liquidity, solvency and capital adequacy (4.3.4, pp. 198–\n200)\nThis is the most important section of the chapter for you.\nLiquidity redefined. In the pure credit economy banks hold no government bonds and no reserves, so the standard liquidity ratio (safe assets to total assets) would always be zero. Lavoie"}, {"pdf": 120, "printed": 93, "ch": 12, "text": "concludes that the only available measure is the proportion of the bank’s own funds: the ratio of loans to own funds, B/OF , or of assets to own funds (p. 198). The capital adequacy ratio as a multiplier. The Basel ratio of own funds to risk-weighted assets was 8% at the time, and is “now quite complex” (p. 199). Some authors argue that the maximum asset-to-own-funds ratio implied by a minimum capital ratio plays the role of the old reserve multiplier (Dow 1996; Borio and Disyatat 2010: “the main exogenous constraint on the expansion of credit is minimum capital requirements”). The example given is Japan after 1990, where low bank net worth was said to prevent new lending. Lavoie’s objections (p. 199). He is sceptical that the constraint binds in general:\n1. the ratios were set so that they bind only for the riskiest banks;\n2. solvent, profitable banks accumulate retained earnings and can issue shares;\n3. the BIS rules allow flexibility in risk-weighting, so accountants can make ratios look good;\n4. banks’ reluctance to accept strict ratios may reflect a wish for a high return on equity rather than fear of being unable to lend. The crisis case (p. 199). If all banks incur large losses that push ratios beyond the limit without making them insolvent, no private agent would buy bank equity, and “the government or the central bank has the responsibility to purchase new equities issued by banks, thus proceeding to partial or full nationalization.” A new loan makes the bank temporarily more risky (pp. 199–200). When a bank lends, loans rise while own funds stay constant, so B/OF rises immediately. Some authors (Wray, Fontana) use this to argue for an upward-sloping credit supply. Lavoie replies that the effect is temporary: the extra net interest income is retained and restores the ratio, so at the macro level there is no upward pressure on lending rates from the loan itself. Compare with Stiglitz (2011) and Tirole\n\nStiglitz (Chapter 10, pp. 614–615, 619) says the capital constraint does bind in a crisis and that this is the problem. Tirole (Chapter 8) derives a lending multiple k = 1/(1 − ρ0 ) from a moral-hazard constraint. Lavoie says the constraint binds mainly for the riskiest banks and in a systemic crisis, where the state steps in. The three agree that capital is the key variable in a crisis; they differ on whether it is a day-to-day brake and on who restores it.\n\n12.5 Credit rationing, creditworthiness and the liquidity preference\nof banks (4.5.4, pp. 245–252)\nTwo meanings of the liquidity preference of banks (p. 245). First, banks choose between loans and safe assets, like households. Second, and the one Lavoie uses, it is the state of banking entrepreneurs’ animal spirits: their confidence about an uncertain future. A high liquidity preference means banks are reluctant to lend to new customers. It links to Keynes’s remark that “there is normally a fringe of unsatisfied borrowers” (1930, p. 364). Horizontalism never ignored creditworthiness (pp. 245–246). Lavoie collects quotations from Le Bourva, Godley and Cripps, Kaldor, Eichner and Moore to show that the original"}, {"pdf": 121, "printed": 94, "ch": 12, "text": "endogenous-money authors said banks lend only to creditworthy borrowers. Kaldor: lending is “limited only by the availability of credit-worthy borrowers”, and in a boom the creditworthiness of borrowers rises with the value of their collateral, so supply and demand move together. Moore’s “three C’s”: credit, collateral and character. Kalecki’s principle of increasing risk (Figure 4.5, p. 247). Across a cross-section of firms, the lending rate rises with the debt-to-equity ratio because of the perceived risk. It is not a time series. Notional versus effective demand for credit (Figure 4.6, pp. 248–250). This is Lavoie’s, via Wolfson (1996), alternative to the Stiglitz–Weiss picture:\n• At a given risk class the supply of credit is horizontal at the lending rate iL = iCB + σB (equation 4.5: the central-bank rate plus a risk and illiquidity premium).\n• There are two demand curves: the notional demand (all who would like to borrow, by their own expectations) and the effective demand (only those whom banks judge creditworthy).\n• The horizontal gap between them at the going rate is credit rationing.\nThe gap widens when (i) the benchmark rate rises: fewer want to borrow and more fail the bank’s criteria; or (ii) the banks’ confidence falls: they raise σB and tighten criteria, so the effective curve shifts left. “Interest-rate spreads, nonprice standards, and credit rationing increase together” (Wolfson, quoted p. 250). Lavoie explicitly notes that the widening gap as the rate rises “may happen because of an adverse selection problem…as New Keynesians have pointed out; or…simply because the higher interest rate makes it more difficult for the borrower to fulfil his or her debt obligations” (p. 249, citing Kalecki). So the S–W mechanism is accepted as possible, but the chief driver he points to is a change in bankers’ confidence. Lavoie--Wolfson\n\nnotional demand\n\n1.15\n\n1.10\nLending rate\n\nBank's expected return ρ(r)\n\nStiglitz--Weiss\n\n1.05\n1.00\n0.95\n0.90\n\nsupply: iL = iCB + σB (horizontal)\n\nrationing\ngap\neffective demand\n(creditworthy only)\n\n0.85\n\nInterest rate r (%)\n\nLoans\n\nFigure 12.1. Two pictures of credit rationing, side by side. Left: Stiglitz–Weiss (Chapter 7): the bank’s return is hump-shaped in the rate; rationing at r∗ is an equilibrium. Right: Lavoie–Wolfson (Figure 4.6): supply is horizontal at iL ; rationing is the gap between notional and effective demand, which widens when confidence falls. Both are schematic and drawn by me, not reproduced from the sources."}, {"pdf": 122, "printed": 95, "ch": 12, "text": "12.6 Financial instability (4.5.5, pp. 252–256)\nLavoie summarises Minsky’s four hypotheses (quoting Minsky 1964, p. 175):\n1. Real behaviour is not independent of financial structure.\n2. The likelihood of a financial crisis depends on the financial structure, which reflects the economy’s past.\n3. In a sustained boom the domain within which the financial structure is stable shrinks.\n4. If a boom is to be broken, it will be by a financial crisis (given no deficiency of demand). His reading: the key idea is the paradox of tranquillity. Success validates optimism; “stability—even in an expansion—is destabilizing” (Minsky, 1975, quoted p. 253); banks forget past events (“myopic hindsight”); they target higher leverage on equity while retained profits grow. At the turning point, a rise in interest rates (by the central bank or from debt burdens) starts a debt-deflation of falling asset prices, margin calls and forced sales, the “Minsky moment” (p. 254), unless the government acts. Lavoie notes that this last step needs an explicit policy decision (Toporowski) so the turning point is not fully endogenous, and he gives alternative views (Gordon, Toporowski) in which firms become more prudent late in a boom (p. 255). He also warns against net measures: gross liabilities matter, since layered financial structures create domino effects (the CDS example, p. 254). Policy. Lavoie draws from Minsky that financial institutions need close regulation and that, if lenders are hard to restrain, restrictions should be put on borrowers: down-payment rules, margin requirements, and credit controls (pp. 255–256). He warns of evasion via disintermediation.\n\n12.7 What is not covered here\n• Section 4.5.6 and 4.6 (shadow banking; stock-flow-consistent accounting) are relevant to Phase 6 (the STAMP€ model is stock-flow consistent). I did not read them in detail.\n• Chapter 6 (growth and financial-fragility models) is not in your folder and I have not covered it.\n\n12.8 Common misreadings\nFive things Lavoie does not say\n\n1. “Banks can lend without limit.” The limits are creditworthy demand and, in his words, the confidence of other banks and of bankers; own funds matter for risk (p. 255).\n2. “Capital requirements are irrelevant.” He says they bind mainly for risky banks and in a systemic crisis, where governments recapitalise.\n3. “Credit rationing never comes from asymmetric information.” He allows it,\nbut says confidence and uncertainty are the main drivers (pp. 249–250).\n4. “Reserves are a multiplier of deposits.” The reserve mechanism is a divisor, if reserves exist at all."}, {"pdf": 123, "printed": 96, "ch": 12, "text": "5. “Minsky predicted the 2008 crisis in every detail.” Lavoie says Minsky was right about financial institutions and households, but he himself stressed firms’ debt, which was not the main problem (p. 255).\n\n12.9 What it means for the thesis\nThis section is my inference about the thesis, not Lavoie’s claim.\nTwo readings of capital adequacy. Your thesis can specify whether capital ratio is (a) an outcome that transition losses erode, or (b) a constraint on lending. Lavoie gives you arguments that (b) is weak in normal times and strong in a systemic crisis. This suggests testing whether the capital ratio affects lending conditionally on being near the minimum or on a stress period. State ownership. Lavoie’s last resort, recapitalisation by the government, is not hypothetical for Indian public-sector banks; it has happened (the government has injected equity into them in recent years; I am stating this from general knowledge, so check figures before citing). The sentence at p. 199 gives you a theoretical anchor for treating the state as the capital provider of last resort, which changes how you interpret ratio shortfalls. Climate and creditworthiness. Under the notional/effective-demand picture, transition risk operates by shifting the effective demand curve left for exposed borrowers (they fail collateral and cash-flow criteria) without any change in asymmetric information. That is an empirically distinguishable story: look for tightening of spreads and non-price standards in exposed sectors, not only for price effects. Flag. The Lavoie–Wolfson picture is a conceptual framework; it has no estimated parameters. Use it to frame hypotheses. Do you need the original? For the claims in Table 12.1, capital adequacy and credit rationing: no, for a lecture or exam. Yes, for quoting. Open the original in these cases:\n• You will quote the capital-adequacy argument. Read pp. 198–200.\n• You will use the credit-rationing diagram. Read pp. 246–250 and Wolfson (1996).\n• You will model the banking system. Read sections 4.3.2–4.3.13 and 4.6 (T-account matrices, SFC).\n• You want Basel details. Lavoie only gestures at them (BIS 2013); use BCBS documents.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ12.1 State three differences in Table 12.1.\nQ12.2 What does “loans create deposits” mean? State the reflux principle."}, {"pdf": 124, "printed": 97, "ch": 12, "text": "Q12.3 What are Minsky’s four hypotheses?\nQ12.4 Define notional and effective demand for credit.\nExplain\nQ12.5 Why does Lavoie measure bank liquidity by own funds in the pure credit economy?\nQ12.6 Give three of Lavoie’s four objections to the claim that capital ratios constrain lending in general.\nQ12.7 Why does he say a new loan raises B/OF only temporarily?\nQ12.8 Why can both a rise in the benchmark rate and a fall in bankers’ confidence widen the rationing gap? Apply\nQ12.9 A bank has own funds Rs 50, loans Rs 500. A new loan of Rs 50 is made. Compute B/OF before and after. If annual retained earnings are Rs 5 and no new lending occurs, what is B/OF a year later?\nQ12.10 Draw (or describe) Figure 12.1 for a case where a regulator announces climate-related tightening: where does each picture predict the change?\nQ12.11 Your data show that spreads on loans to coal-linked firms rose while volumes fell. Which interpretation (S–W or Lavoie–Wolfson) does this favour? What additional evidence would separate them? Viva\nQ12.12 “If banks create credit ex nihilo, capital adequacy ratios are pointless.” Respond.\nQ12.13 “Lavoie says rationing comes from confidence, Stiglitz–Weiss from information. Which is right?” Answer with reference to what each can explain and what each can be tested on.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA12.1 E.g. money endogenous versus exogenous; credits make deposits versus deposits allow credits; creators of credit versus intermediaries; rationing from confidence versus information.\nA12.2 The bank creates the deposit when it grants the loan. Reflux: as loans are repaid, the deposits created by lending return and are extinguished.\nA12.3 See the list in section 4.5.5 above.\nA12.4 Notional: all who would like to borrow at a given rate. Effective: only those whom banks consider creditworthy.\nA12.5 With no government bonds or reserves, safe-assets ratios are always zero, so own funds are all that is left.\nA12.6 Any three: ratios bind only for the riskiest banks; retained earnings and share issues; accounting flexibility in risk weights; banks’ reluctance reflects return on equity."}, {"pdf": 125, "printed": 98, "ch": 12, "text": "A12.7 Higher loans bring higher net interest income, retained earnings add to own funds and bring the ratio back.\nA12.8 A higher rate reduces notional demand and makes more borrowers fail the criteria (or adverse selection); lower confidence raises the risk premium and shifts the effective curve left.\nA12.9 Before: 500/50 = 10. After: 550/50 = 11. A year later: 550/55 = 10.\nA12.10 S–W: the hump moves down (or r∗ shifts left) if safe borrowers are more affected; Lavoie–Wolfson: effective demand shifts left for exposed borrowers, widening the gap and raising σB .\nA12.11 Rising spreads with falling volumes are consistent with both; they favour neither alone. To separate: S–W predicts that raising the rate worsens the pool (higher default among those who remain), while L–W predicts tighter non-price standards (collateral, covenants) and rationing that follows confidence shocks rather than rate changes.\nA12.12 Not pointless: Lavoie says capital matters for risk and binds for risky banks and in systemic crises; the question is which constraint is binding, not whether credit is created by lending.\nA12.13 They are not mutually exclusive. S–W gives a micro mechanism that holds even with stable confidence; Lavoie emphasises that freezes in crises come with rising uncertainty and the shift in bankers’ expectations. Test: whether rationing varies with the information environment (e.g. disclosure mandates) or with confidence indicators."}, {"pdf": 126, "printed": 99, "ch": 13, "text": "CHAPTER 13\n\nPhase 2 Synthesis, Lecture Outline and\nTest\n13.1 Five readings side by side\nFama (1970)\n\nStiglitz–Weiss Tirole (2006)\n(1981)\n\nStiglitz (2011) Lavoie (2014)\n\nQuestion\n\nDo prices reflect Why may credit How much can a Why did macro What is postinformation? be rationed? firm raise from models fail in Keynesian outsiders? 2008? economics, and its money and credit view?\n\nType\n\nReview of the- Formal model\nory and tests\n\nInformation\n\nPublic or pri- Asymmetric:\nMoral hazard: Asymmetric;\nFundamental\nvate; assumed bank cannot see effort unobserv- central to the uncertainty\nto be priced\nproject risk\nable\ncritique\n\nTextbook model Lecture-essay\n\nSurvey/textbook\n\nKey variable Information set Interest rate r; Pledgeable in- Bank net worth; Creditworthiness; collateral come; net worth leverage bankers’ confiA dence Role banks\n\nof None\n\nMaximise\nex- Intermediaries\nAmplifiers via Creators\npected return\nwith monitoring capital\ncon- credit\nstraints\n\nCause\nof n/a\nrationing /\nfreeze\n\nAdverse\nlection\nincentives\n\nSchool\n(Lavoie)\n\nOrthodox dis- Mainstream\nsent\n(New\nKeynesian)\n\nMainstream\n\nMain use in Event-study\nthesis\nmethod; limits\nof market-based\nmeasures\n\nse- Moral hazard\nand\n\nWhy\ncapital Capital\nand collateral tiplier;\nmatter for lend- overhang\ning\n\nNet-worth\nLoss of\ncollapse, uncer- dence\ntainty\nOrthodox\nsent\n\nconfi-\n\ndis- Heterodox\n\nmul- Amplification;\ndebt countercyclical\nrules\n\nFraming; creditrationing alternative\n\nTable 13.1. Phase 2 readings compared. The last-but-one row uses Lavoie’s classification (Chapter 11). The Tirole row reflects only the chapter covered in Chapter 8."}, {"pdf": 127, "printed": 100, "ch": 13, "text": "Fama (1970)\n\nHow financial markets are seen\n\nStable,\nefficient\n\nTirole (2006)\n\nStiglitz--Weiss (1981)\n\nStiglitz (2011)\nLavoie (2014)\nKeynes, Minsky\n\nUnstable,\nfragile\n\nRisk: known\ndistributions\n\nFundamental\nuncertainty\n\nWhat the future is assumed to be like\n\nFigure 13.1. A positioning map for the Phase 2 readings. Horizontal axis: what the author assumes about the future (risk with known distributions, to fundamental uncertainty). Vertical axis: how stable the author thinks financial markets are. The positions are my own judgement from the readings, drawn to help you discuss them; they are not measurements and no author would necessarily accept their place.\n\n13.2 What Phase 2 gives the thesis\n1. Why bank capital matters for lending. Stiglitz–Weiss and Tirole give micro reasons (adverse selection, moral hazard, pledgeable income) why a borrower’s or a bank’s net worth limits what can be lent. Stiglitz (2011) scales this up to the economy. Lavoie says the constraint is weaker in ordinary times and strongest in a systemic crisis.\n2. A method. Fama gives the event-study and market-model machinery, and, crucially, the warning that every test is a joint test.\n3. Two accounts of credit rationing. Information-based (Chapters 7, 8) and confidencebased (Chapter 12). They suggest different empirical signatures (Chapter 12, applied questions).\n4. Policy. Countercyclical capital standards (Stiglitz) and credit controls and borrower-side rules (Lavoie/Minsky) as alternatives to purely price-based tools.\n5. Positioning. A vocabulary (orthodox dissent, heterodox dissent) to explain to an examiner where the thesis stands.\n\n13.3 Positioning the thesis\nMy suggestion, not any author’s claim. Build on Phase 1’s separation of levels (motivation, measurement, interpretation; Phase 1, Chapter 6): Lavoie’s own remark (Chapter 11) that orthodox dissenters and post-Keynesians often reach"}, {"pdf": 128, "printed": 101, "ch": 13, "text": "Task in the thesis\n\nSource from Phase 2\n\nClaim you make\n\nWhy capital ade- Stiglitz–Weiss; Tirole; Stiglitz (2011)\nquacy matters for\nlending\nHow shocks amplify\n\nCapital and collateral are channels\nthrough which losses reduce credit.\n\nStiglitz (2011) Table 10.2; Lavoie on Transition shocks can feed back\nMinsky\nthrough bank net worth and asset\nprices.\n\nWhy not assume mar- Fama; Lavoie (non-ergodicity)\nkets price it correctly\n\nMarket measures reflect beliefs; efficiency is tested, not assumed.\n\nWhat kind of expla- Lavoie 4.5.4 versus S–W\nnation of rationing\n\nState which you test; accept that both\nmay operate.\n\nTable 13.2. Where each Phase 2 source is used.\n\nsimilar policy conclusions by different routes helps you here: you can use mainstream-dissent micro (Stiglitz–Weiss, Tirole) for mechanisms while borrowing the post-Keynesian view of uncertainty to motivate scenarios.\n\n13.4 Corrections and gaps in Phase 2\n1. Lavoie Chapter 6 is not covered. Your reading plan lists Chapters 1, 2 and 6; your folder holds 1, 2 and 4. Chapter 6 (growth with financial fragility, Minskyan models) is the part that could be built into your model. Send it and I will add a chapter.\n2. Tirole Chapter 6 (adverse selection) is not covered. Phase 1’s About page said Phase 2 would use Chapters 3 and 6. Chapter 8 covers Chapter 3 only; adverse selection in the Stiglitz–Weiss setting is covered by Chapter 7.\n3. Nikolaidi and Stockhammer (2017) and Nikolaidi (2021) are in your plan but not in your folder. They are Post-Keynesian stock-flow models of finance and climate; I have not read them and do not describe them. Download them if you want a Phase 2 chapter on them.\n4. Page citations. Lavoie’s printed page equals the PDF page minus 15. Stiglitz–Weiss, Fama and Stiglitz (2011) pages are the journal’s printed pages. Tirole’s are the book’s printed pages.\n5. Items from general knowledge are labelled: the later criticisms of Stiglitz–Weiss (Chapter 7), the later debate on efficient markets (Chapter 9) and the recapitalisation of Indian public-sector banks (Chapter 12).\n\n13.5 A 90-minute lecture on Phase 2\n13.6 Phase 2 test\nAllow 90 minutes and attempt without the notes. The answers follow.\nRecall"}, {"pdf": 129, "printed": 102, "ch": 13, "text": "Minutes\n\nSegment\n\nContent\n\nBoard / slide\n\n0–10\n\nHook\n\n“A bank has a good borrower who offers a Two\nhigher interest rate. Should it lend more?”\nyes/no.\n\n10–30\n\nStiglitz–Weiss\n\nSafe and risky projects; hump-shaped bank Figure 7.1;\nreturn; why r∗ < rm . Numerical example ble 7.2.\n(27.5%).\n\n30–45\n\nTirole\n\nMoral hazard, pledgeable income, k = 1/(1 − Figure 8.1.\nρ0 ).\n\n45–55\n\nFama\n\nInformation sets; joint hypothesis; event study. Figures 9.1, 9.2.\n\n55–70\n\nStiglitz (2011)\n\nWhat the Standard Model leaves out; the Figure 10.1.\nbank-capital loop.\n\n70–82\n\nLavoie\n\nCredits make deposits; notional versus effec- Figure 12.1.\ntive demand; capital adequacy.\n\n82–90\n\nThesis link and close\n\nTable 13.2. Question: “Which would you test, One slide.\nand what would count against it?”\n\ncolumns:\nTa-\n\nTable 13.3. Lecture outline for Phase 2.\n\nQ13.1 In Stiglitz–Weiss, state the definition of credit rationing and why the bank’s expected return is not monotonic in the interest rate.\nQ13.2 In Tirole’s fixed-investment model, what is pledgeable income, and what is the incentivecompatibility condition?\nQ13.3 Define the three forms of market efficiency and give one test for each.\nQ13.4 What does Lavoie say is the cause of credit rationing? What is the mainstream answer? Explain\nQ13.5 Why is collateral not a free fix for adverse selection in Stiglitz–Weiss (Theorems 9–12)?\nQ13.6 Why does Tirole’s equity multiplier k = 1/(1 − ρ0 ) rise with pledgeable income per unit of investment ρ0 ? What is the connection with capital adequacy ratios?\nQ13.7 Why does Stiglitz say rigid capital standards may destabilise? Use the multiplier arithmetic.\nQ13.8 Why is the joint-hypothesis problem a problem for CRISK-style measures?\nApply\nQ13.9 Using the S–W example (safe p = .9, R = 1.9; risky p = .5, R = 2.4), compute the rate at which the borrower is indifferent between the projects, and the bank’s return per rupee at r = 20% and r = 30%.\nQ13.10 A firm has I = 100, R = 150, pH = .8, pL = .5, B = 15. Compute NPV, minimum net worth Ā and agency rent.\nQ13.11 You regress bank lending on capital ratio and find a significant effect only when ratios are within 1 percentage point of the minimum. Which authors does that support, and why?"}, {"pdf": 130, "printed": 103, "ch": 13, "text": "Viva\nQ13.12 “Credit rationing is a theoretical curiosity; real banks just charge a higher rate for riskier loans.” Answer using Stiglitz–Weiss, Lavoie’s Kalecki figure and the credit-line argument.\nQ13.13 “Your thesis uses Fama’s event study and Lavoie’s non-ergodicity. Isn’t that incoherent?” Respond in six sentences.\nQ13.14 “Is the Indian public-sector bank an example of Stiglitz’s model, Lavoie’s, or neither?” Give a position with two pieces of evidence you would collect.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA13.1 Rationing: some borrowers get no loan even though they would pay the quoted rate, or some obtain less than they wish, and no rise in the rate clears the market. A higher rate drives out safer borrowers (adverse selection) and induces riskier projects (incentive), so the pool worsens and the bank’s return can fall.\nA13.2 Pledgeable income: the part of project returns that can be promised to outsiders without destroying the borrower’s incentive, pH (R − B/∆p). IC: (∆p)Rb ≥ B.\nA13.3 Weak: past prices; test serial correlation or filter rules. Semi-strong: all public information; event study. Strong: all information, including private; mutual-fund performance and insider trading studies.\nA13.4 Lavoie: loss of confidence and changes in bankers’ liquidity preference under uncertainty (with asymmetric information allowed as a minor cause). Mainstream: asymmetric information.\nA13.5 Theorem 12: raising the collateral requirement drives out less wealthy, more cautious borrowers, so the average borrower who remains is riskier; this selection effect can outweigh the direct gain to the bank from collateral (Theorem 11). Theorem 10 adds that, with decreasing absolute risk aversion, wealthier borrowers undertake riskier projects, so collateral screens by wealth, not by safety.\nA13.6 ρ0 is what can be pledged to outsiders per unit invested. The larger it is (a lower agency cost B), the more of each unit outsiders will finance, so each unit of own funds supports k = 1/(1−ρ0 ) units of investment. Capital adequacy is the bank-level analogue: capital divided by assets caps the lending multiple at its inverse.\nA13.7 With 8% capital, each rupee of capital supports Rs 12.5 of assets; a loss of Rs 10 requires a cut of Rs 125 unless capital is raised, which reduces asset prices and capital again.\nA13.8 CRISK uses equity returns and so assumes prices carry information about beliefs; if prices do not reflect Knightian-uncertain climate risk, the measure is conditional on market beliefs. Any test of its content is joint with a model of normal returns.\nA13.9 1 + r∗ = (1.71 − 1.20)/0.4 = 1.275, so r∗ = 27.5%. Bank return per rupee: 1.08 at 20%;\n0.65 at 30%.\nA13.10 NPV = pH R−I = .8×150−100 = 20. Ā = pH B/∆p−(pH R−I) = .8×15/.3−20 = 20. Agency rent = pH B/∆p = 40."}, {"pdf": 131, "printed": 104, "ch": 13, "text": "A13.11 It supports the view that the constraint matters mainly for banks near the minimum (Lavoie’s first objection) and in stress (Stiglitz). Under Tirole, it would show up as a multiplier limit for low-net-worth lenders.\nA13.12 Stiglitz–Weiss: a higher rate may lower the bank’s expected return, so the bank prefers rationing to repricing. Lavoie’s Figure 4.5 (Kalecki): the rate does rise with leverage across firms, so price differentiation exists, but it coexists with limits on credit lines, which are contracts specifying maximum amounts. Rationing is about quantity limits at the offered price.\nA13.13 Not incoherent if the levels are stated: Fama’s method is a measurement tool that does not require believing efficiency; the event study can be read as testing whether information is incorporated under a specified model; non-ergodicity is motivation for scenarios. Be explicit that results are conditional on the model of normal returns and on the sample period. A joint test is exactly what Fama says every test is.\nA13.14 A defensible answer: partly Stiglitz (capital constraints and state recapitalisation) and partly Lavoie (government as capital provider; weak market discipline); neither fully, because public-sector banks face directed lending and implicit guarantees. Evidence: (i) lending growth against capital ratio near the minimum; (ii) spread and non-price standards by sector before and after a policy or disclosure change; (iii) recapitalisation episodes."}], "cards": [{"id": "7.1", "ch": 7, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Give the paper’s two definitions of credit rationing. Which one is redlining?", "a": "(a) Among apparently identical applicants some get loans and rejected ones would not get one even at a higher interest rate; (b) identifiable groups cannot borrow at any rate given the supply of credit, though they could with more supply. (b) is redlining."}, {"id": "7.2", "ch": 7, "lv": "Recall", "q": "In one sentence each, state Theorems 1, 2 and 3.", "a": "T1: for a given r there is a critical risk θ̂ such that a firm borrows iff θ > θ̂. T2: θ̂ rises with r. T3: the bank’s expected return falls as loan riskiness rises."}, {"id": "7.3", "ch": 7, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What is the “bank-optimal” interest rate, and why is it not the market-clearing rate?", "a": "The r that maximises the bank’s expected return per unit lent, ρ(r). It differs from rm (where demand equals supply) because beyond r∗ higher rates worsen the pool or the behaviour enough to lower ρ."}, {"id": "7.4", "ch": 7, "lv": "Recall", "q": "According to Corollary 1, what happens to the interest rate when the supply of funds increases while rationing exists?", "a": "The rate stays at r∗ ; only excess demand falls, until it reaches zero."}, {"id": "7.5", "ch": 7, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does a mean-preserving spread make a project more valuable to the borrower and less valuable to the bank? Use the shapes in Table 7.1.", "a": "The borrower’s payoff is convex in R (she keeps the upside, loses little on the downside), so spread helps; the bank’s is concave (capped upside, exposed downside), so spread hurts."}, {"id": "7.6", "ch": 7, "lv": "Explain", "q": "In Table 7.3 the bank’s return at r = 30% is lower than at r = 20%. Explain the whole chain from the interest rate to the bank’s return, naming the effect.", "a": "Direct effect: more income per repaid rupee. Adverse incentive effect (Theorem 8): the higher rate makes the firm switch to the risky project, with success probability 0.5 instead of 0.9, and this outweighs the higher rate."}, {"id": "7.7", "ch": 7, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the paper say raising collateral requirements can reduce the bank’s return? Distinguish the direct effect from the selection effect.", "a": "Direct: collateral raises the return from any given borrower. Selection: the less wealthy, less risk-tolerant borrowers drop out, so the rest are riskier; beyond a critical collateral the second effect dominates."}, {"id": "7.8", "ch": 7, "lv": "Explain", "q": "The authors say the law of supply and demand is “not a law”. Under what assumption would it hold?", "a": "When prices have no sorting or incentive effects, i.e., when the quality of the thing traded does not depend on the price."}, {"id": "7.9", "ch": 7, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Design a numerical example like Table 7.2 in which the safe project is chosen up to r∗ = 15%. (Hint: use 1 + r∗ = (pb Rb − pa Ra )/(pb − pa ).)", "a": "Example: take pb = 0.9, pa = 0.5. Need pb Rb − pa Ra = 0.4 × 1.15 = 0.46. With Ra = 2.4 (pa Ra = 1.20), pb Rb = 1.66, so Rb = 1.844. Check the firm is indifferent at r = 15%."}, {"id": "7.10", "ch": 7, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A bank is considering raising the lending rate on a group of thermal-power borrowers whose revenues depend on policy. State which of the paper’s three mechanisms could make this unprofitable, and what observable signs you would look for in loan data.", "a": "Adverse selection (safer thermal borrowers leave), incentive (borrower switches to riskier operations to earn the return needed to pay the rate), and collateral effects. Signs: rising non-performing assets after rate increases, declining share of high-rated borrowers in the applicant pool, and refusals of applicants who offer higher rates."}, {"id": "7.11", "ch": 7, "lv": "Apply", "q": "How would you tell, empirically, whether a fall in lending to a sector is caused by rationing or by fall in demand? What does the paper imply about the interest rate in each case?", "a": "Under demand fall, the quoted rate would fall and approval rates among applicants stay stable; under rationing, rates stay flat or sticky while approvals fall and applicants who look identical are treated differently. The paper implies that in the second case the interest rate does not move to clear the market."}, {"id": "7.12", "ch": 7, "lv": "Viva", "q": "An examiner says: “Your thesis uses the credit-rationing literature, but in India lending rates are often set by regulation, so rationing is just regulation.” Give a reply of three sentences.", "a": "Regulation can explain a cap, but the paper’s rationing arises in an unregulated competitive market. Regulated rates may add to it. The thesis should therefore test for rationing controlling for rate rules and say which source dominates."}, {"id": "7.13", "ch": 7, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Stiglitz–Weiss is about risk, but your thesis says climate is uncertainty. Isn’t that inconsistent?” How do you answer without dropping either?", "a": "Different layers. S–W’s risk is a known distribution with hidden types, an information problem; climate outcomes add Type 3 uncertainty about the distributions themselves. Both can hold: information frictions amplify the effect of uncertainty on credit supply."}, {"id": "8.1", "ch": 8, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the incentive constraint (ICb ) and the pledgeable income. Which is the quantity that limits borrowing?", "a": "(∆p)Rb ≥ B; pledgeable income P = pH (R − B/∆p). Pledgeable income limits borrowing because lenders can receive at most this."}, {"id": "8.2", "ch": 8, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Write the threshold Ā and name its two parts.", "a": "Ā = pH B/∆p − (pH R − I): the agency rent minus the NPV."}, {"id": "8.3", "ch": 8, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Define the equity multiplier k in terms of ρ0 . What happens to k when the private benefit rises?", "a": "k = 1/(1 − ρ0 ) with ρ0 = pH R − pH B/∆p. A higher B lowers ρ0 and so lowers k."}, {"id": "8.4", "ch": 8, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Name two reasons that, according to Tirole, a firm may be credit constrained.", "a": "Low cash on hand; high agency cost (large private benefit and a low likelihood ratio); also a high market interest rate, weak investor protection (any two)."}, {"id": "8.5", "ch": 8, "lv": "Explain", "q": "In Table 8.2, why can the entrepreneur with A = 15 not simply promise to repay a higher interest rate to get the loan?", "a": "Because a higher promised share leaves her with less than B/∆p in the success state, so she shirks and the lenders’ expected income falls. Her offer is not credible."}, {"id": "8.6", "ch": 8, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is the shadow value of equity greater than 1? What would happen to it if the project were riskless?", "a": "Because an extra rupee unlocks k rupees of investment each earning surplus. If the project were riskless in the sense that there were no moral hazard (B = 0), pledgeable income would equal the whole expected return, and equity would be worth only 1."}, {"id": "8.7", "ch": 8, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does an extra source of finance for a “deepening” investment hurt initial lenders even though their formal claim is untouched?", "a": "The new financing changes her incentives (more leverage, less stake) and so lowers the probability of success on the original loan; the lenders lose through the incentive channel even though their share in success is unchanged."}, {"id": "8.8", "ch": 8, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Compare the roles of the interest rate in Stiglitz–Weiss and in Tirole. Where does each appear in the mechanism?", "a": "S–W: the rate selects types and induces risk-taking; the hump in the bank’s return gives rationing. Tirole: the rate acts through the borrower’s stake; rationing arises from the net worth constraint; in effect a high rate is equivalent to low cash."}, {"id": "8.9", "ch": 8, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Using R = 1.4, pH = 0.85, pL = 0.55, B = 0.15 per unit, compute ρ0 , k and d. Check that ρ1 > 1 > ρ0 and that pL R + B < 1.", "a": "∆p = 0.30, so B/∆p = 0.5 and ρ0 = 0.85(1.4 − 0.5) = 0.765; k = 1/0.235 ≈ 4.26; d ≈ 3.26. ρ1 = 0.85 × 1.4 = 1.19 > 1 > 0.765 ✓and pL R + B = 0.77 + 0.15 = 0.92 < 1 ✓."}, {"id": "8.10", "ch": 8, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A borrower has A = 100 and k = 3. A shock cuts her net worth by 20%. What happens to her maximum investment? What if k = 6?", "a": "A 20% fall: A = 80. With k = 3, max investment falls from 300 to 240 (a fall of 60). With k = 6, from 600 to 480 (fall of 120). Higher multiplier, bigger amplification."}, {"id": "8.11", "ch": 8, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A bank lends to a coal-power firm that owes legacy debt D. State, in terms of A, Ā and D, when the firm cannot fund a profitable solar addition (Section 3.3.1).", "a": "If A > Ā but A − D < Ā (with A − D ≥ 0), the solar project is rationed because the legacy claim absorbs the net worth the new lenders need."}, {"id": "8.12", "ch": 8, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Tirole assumes risk neutrality and zero interest. How can that say anything about banks under Basel norms?” Answer in four sentences.", "a": "Model scope: it concerns an incentive problem, not Basel rules. What carries over is the structure of “lending limited to a multiple of own funds, with the multiple set by agency cost”. A bank’s regulator ratio plays a similar role. Careful claims should say “analogous”, not “identical”."}, {"id": "8.13", "ch": 8, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“If rationed projects are positive NPV, why don’t other lenders step in?” Give the model’s answer.", "a": "In this model all lenders face the same agency problem and the same pledgeable income, so a new lender is in the same position as an old one: no one can profitably lend more. The positive NPV is not capturable."}, {"id": "9.1", "ch": 9, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State Fama’s definition of an efficient market and the three information sets that give the three forms.", "a": "Prices at every time “fully reflect” available information. Weak: past prices; semi-strong: all public information; strong: all information including private."}, {"id": "9.2", "ch": 9, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What is a fair game? A submartingale? A random walk? Which is the weakest?", "a": "Fair game: surprises have zero conditional mean given the information. Submartingale: expected next price ≥ current price. Random walk: successive returns independent and identically distributed. The fair game (expected return) model is the weakest."}, {"id": "9.3", "ch": 9, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Name the three market conditions Fama says are sufficient for efficiency. Are they necessary?", "a": "No transaction costs; all information free to all; all agree on its implications. No, they are sufficient not necessary (p. 388)."}, {"id": "9.4", "ch": 9, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What did Jensen find about mutual funds? What are the two groups with documented strong-form advantages?", "a": "Funds in aggregate do not beat the market line after expenses (89 of 115 below the line net of loads); no consistent individual winners. Documented strong-form advantages: NYSE specialists and corporate insiders."}, {"id": "9.5", "ch": 9, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Fama say that statistically significant serial correlation does not refute efficiency? Use the 0.06 example.", "a": "With thousands of observations small correlations are many standard errors from zero, but 0.062 = 0.0036: it explains 0.36% of variation, too little to cover costs. Significance and economic importance are different."}, {"id": "9.6", "ch": 9, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain why the market model is needed to test the semi-strong form. What do the cumulative average residuals show if the market is efficient?", "a": "The market model gives the normal relationship between a stock and the market, so that residuals measure the part due to the event. If efficient, Um jumps at the event and is flat afterwards."}, {"id": "9.7", "ch": 9, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Fama say that a test of efficiency is always a joint test? What is the second hypothesis?", "a": "Because the claim “prices fully reflect Φt ” only has content after specifying the equilibrium expected-return model (CAPM, etc.). If the test fails you cannot tell which part is wrong."}, {"id": "9.8", "ch": 9, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why can a market be efficient even if transaction costs are large (p. 388)?", "a": "Because efficiency requires only that when transactions occur the price reflects the information; costs reduce the number of trades, not the information content of prices (unless they block transactions altogether)."}, {"id": "9.9", "ch": 9, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A stock has α̂ = 0, β̂ = 1.2. On an announcement day the market returns +1.0% and the stock returns +2.5%. Compute the abnormal return. If the next four days have abnormal returns of +0.2%, −0.1%, +0.3%, 0.0%, what is the cumulative abnormal return over the five days?", "a": "AR = 2.5 − (0 + 1.2 × 1.0) = 1.3%. CAR = 1.3 + 0.2 − 0.1 + 0.3 + 0.0 = 1.7%."}, {"id": "9.10", "ch": 9, "lv": "Apply", "q": "You find that bank stock returns on RBI climate-circular days are not different from zero. List two reasons other than “banks are unaffected by climate risk”.", "a": "(i) Announcement was anticipated (Waud found anticipation); (ii) the window contaminated by other news; (iii) thin trading and noise, or low power; (iv) the circular is not new information. Any two. √"}, {"id": "9.11", "ch": 9, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Suppose your estimated daily serial correlation for an index is 0.04 with N = 2,500. Compute the approximate standard error and say whether it is significant and economically meaningful.", "a": "SE ≈ 1/ 2500 = 0.02. 0.04 is two standard errors, so borderline significant; but 0.042 = 0.16% of variance is economically negligible."}, {"id": "9.12", "ch": 9, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Fama’s efficient markets contradict Knight and Keynes: markets cannot be efficient under Knightian uncertainty.” Respond in four sentences.", "a": "Fama’s model presupposes a known return distribution given the information, i.e., risk, not Knightian uncertainty. Under Type 3 uncertainty there is no accepted equilibrium model, so a test is not possible or not informative. This is a limit of the efficient-market test, not a refutation of Knight. A defensible position is to use markets as a source of information about beliefs, not as proof of correct pricing."}, {"id": "9.13", "ch": 9, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Your thesis uses CRISK, which is based on equity returns. Why should equity returns reveal anything about climate risk if markets do not price it?” How do you answer?", "a": "Equity returns reveal market beliefs and bank exposure to systemic shocks, even if they do not price climate risk correctly. CRISK is conditional on a stated market decline, not a claim that prices are right. If prices underreact, CRISK is a lower bound on a belief-based measure."}, {"id": "10.1", "ch": 10, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does Stiglitz mean by the “Standard Model”? Name four things absent from it.", "a": "The DSGE-based consensus model. Absent: information asymmetries, a real financial sector, debt and bankruptcy, heterogeneity (also credit as opposed to money, endogenous shocks)."}, {"id": "10.2", "ch": 10, "lv": "Recall", "q": "List the three puzzles of section 3 and one mechanism for each.", "a": "Origin: endogenous bubbles fed by collateral lending. Amplification: financial accelerator or bank capital constraints. Persistence: slow balance-sheet repair or Fisher debt deflation."}, {"id": "10.3", "ch": 10, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What are the four hypotheses of section 4?", "a": "Risk, information, credit markets, structural transformation."}, {"id": "10.4", "ch": 10, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does Stiglitz say about rigid versus countercyclical capital adequacy standards (p. 619)?", "a": "Rigid standards can be destabilising; countercyclical ones (macroprudential regulation) can be stabilising."}, {"id": "10.5", "ch": 10, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain the financial accelerator in a loop of five steps, using a bank rather than a firm as the borrower.", "a": "Asset prices fall; bank capital falls; capital rule forces lending to fall (by a multiple); investment and demand fall; asset prices fall further. The bank takes the place of the firm because the constraint applies to its net worth."}, {"id": "10.6", "ch": 10, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the representative-agent assumption make bankruptcy impossible to model?", "a": "With one agent there is no one to owe money to; a loan is just a transfer from the agent to itself, so default has no meaning."}, {"id": "10.7", "ch": 10, "lv": "Explain", "q": "What is Grossman–Stiglitz’s point about information, and why does it contradict the strong form of Fama’s hypothesis?", "a": "If prices fully reflected all information, nobody would pay to gather information, so nobody would make prices informative; hence perfect efficiency is impossible. The strong form requires prices to reflect even private information."}, {"id": "10.8", "ch": 10, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Lavoie call Stiglitz an orthodox dissenter and not a heterodox economist?", "a": "Because his critique keeps optimisation, equilibrium and information-based frictions; Lavoie calls the heterodox position one that rejects the core methodology (Chapter 11)."}, {"id": "10.9", "ch": 10, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A bank has capital Rs 100 and risk-weighted assets Rs 1,000 (ratio 10%), minimum 9%. A shock wipes out Rs 20 of capital. If it cannot raise capital, by how much must it shrink risk-weighted assets to restore 9%? Compare this with a case where the minimum is lowered to 8% in the downturn.", "a": "Required assets = 100 − 20 = 80 of capital /0.09 = Rs 888.9; shrink by about Rs 111 (from 1,000). With 8%: 80/0.08 = 1,000, so no shrinkage. The countercyclical relaxation removes the forced cut."}, {"id": "10.10", "ch": 10, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Pick one Indian public-sector bank. List which items from Table 10.2 apply to it in a transition-risk scenario and which do not (for example, wholesale funding).", "a": "Answers differ by bank; a sound answer separates channels that apply (collateral values, borrower net worth, capital ratio) from those that do not (securitisation, shadow banking, wholesale funding, which is small for public-sector banks)."}, {"id": "10.11", "ch": 10, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Suppose a regulator requires banks to build extra climate-related provisions during good years and lets them draw those down in a stress year. Use the paper to explain the likely effect, and contrast it with a rule that demands extra provisions only after losses appear.", "a": "Building buffers in good years and releasing them in stress is the countercyclical direction Stiglitz favours (p. 619): it avoids forced shrinkage when capital is scarce. A rule that demands extra provisions only after losses appear is procyclical and would deepen the loop in Figure 10.1."}, {"id": "10.12", "ch": 10, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Stiglitz offers no model, so his paper cannot support a thesis.” Respond.", "a": "The paper offers mechanisms and a list of ingredients, which suffices for framing a research question and choosing variables; the estimation comes from elsewhere (Phases 4 to 6). A thesis needs a theoretical framing, not a single model."}, {"id": "10.13", "ch": 10, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“If markets were as irrational as Stiglitz says, central banks and regulators (also human) would be just as bad.” How might Stiglitz and a post-Keynesian answer differently?", "a": "Stiglitz: policy-makers face different incentives and information; institutions and rules (countercyclical standards, resolution regimes) can correct errors. Post-Keynesian: fundamental uncertainty means no one can compute the right answer, so rules should be simple and robust, and the state acts as lender and spender of last resort."}, {"id": "11.1", "ch": 11, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Distinguish mainstream, orthodox dissenters and heterodox dissenters. Where does Lavoie put Stiglitz?", "a": "Mainstream: textbook consensus. Orthodox dissenters: critical but use mainstream methods. Heterodox dissenters: reject the core method. Stiglitz: orthodox dissenter (p. 10, p. 71)."}, {"id": "11.2", "ch": 11, "lv": "Recall", "q": "List four post-Keynesian presuppositions that Lavoie calls specific to the school.", "a": "Any four of: effective demand in the long run; investment determines saving; institutions matter; monetized economy; historical time; fundamental uncertainty; non-ergodicity."}, {"id": "11.3", "ch": 11, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Name Gigerenzer’s four kinds of rationality and place Fama’s efficient-market hypothesis.", "a": "Unbounded (a), bounded optimisation (b), cognitive illusions (c), environmentconsistent/ecological (d). Efficient markets: (a)."}, {"id": "11.4", "ch": 11, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What is the difference between substantive and procedural uncertainty?", "a": "Substantive: lack of information. Procedural: inability to process it (competence gap)."}, {"id": "11.5", "ch": 11, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does non-ergodicity leave “little room” for rational expectations?", "a": "If past averages do not predict the future, agents cannot know the true distribution, which rational expectations requires."}, {"id": "11.6", "ch": 11, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is the “investment determines saving” claim linked to the principle of effective demand?", "a": "Investment is decided by expected profit, not by households’ saving; income adjusts so that saving equals investment, which is effective demand at work."}, {"id": "11.7", "ch": 11, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Lavoie say that the new behavioural economics is orthodox dissent?", "a": "It keeps unbounded rationality as the benchmark and treats deviations as errors rather than rational responses to uncertainty."}, {"id": "11.8", "ch": 11, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain, using the dropout-rate example, why a model that fits the past best may predict worst.", "a": "Regression exploits every detail of the first half, including noise; on new data the noise is different, so accuracy falls. Simple rules ignore noise."}, {"id": "11.9", "ch": 11, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Classify these as orthodox, orthodox-dissent or heterodox-dissent: (i) a DSGE model with a financial accelerator; (ii) a stock-flow-consistent model with endogenous money; (iii) Stiglitz–Weiss; (iv) a CAPM event study.", "a": "(i) orthodox dissent; (ii) heterodox; (iii) orthodox dissent; (iv) orthodox (mainstream finance)."}, {"id": "11.10", "ch": 11, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Your panel regression on bank capital fits well in-sample. Give two tests that would reveal overfitting, in the spirit of Gigerenzer.", "a": "Out-of-sample prediction on a hold-out period or bank group; leave-one-bank-out crossvalidation; stability across sub-samples; fewer regressors."}, {"id": "11.11", "ch": 11, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Give an example from Indian banking of a rule or habit that works under procedural uncertainty, and say what a bounded-optimisation model would add.", "a": "E.g. a rule of lending only against collateral of at least some multiple, or a standard haircut. A bounded-optimisation model would add the cost of gathering information about each borrower."}, {"id": "11.12", "ch": 11, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Post-Keynesian theory is not testable because uncertainty is unmeasurable.” Reply, using Lavoie on econometrics and Heiner on regularities.", "a": "Lavoie: post-Keynesians use econometrics, with caution (p. 37); Heiner: uncertainty creates rule-based regularities that can be studied. Unmeasurable is not unobservable."}, {"id": "11.13", "ch": 11, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“You use both Stiglitz–Weiss and post-Keynesian ideas. Isn’t that inconsistent?” Answer in five sentences.", "a": "Not necessarily: they work at different levels. Stiglitz–Weiss explains a specific market mechanism (rationing) and is testable; the post-Keynesian view frames the macro uncertainty. The thesis should state which claims it takes from which, and which would survive if the other were dropped. Lavoie himself says orthodox dissenters and postKeynesians reach similar policy conclusions by different routes."}, {"id": "12.1", "ch": 12, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State three differences in Table 12.1.", "a": "E.g. money endogenous versus exogenous; credits make deposits versus deposits allow credits; creators of credit versus intermediaries; rationing from confidence versus information."}, {"id": "12.2", "ch": 12, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does “loans create deposits” mean? State the reflux principle.", "a": "The bank creates the deposit when it grants the loan. Reflux: as loans are repaid, the deposits created by lending return and are extinguished."}, {"id": "12.3", "ch": 12, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What are Minsky’s four hypotheses?", "a": "See the list in section 4.5.5 above."}, {"id": "12.4", "ch": 12, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Define notional and effective demand for credit.", "a": "Notional: all who would like to borrow at a given rate. Effective: only those whom banks consider creditworthy."}, {"id": "12.5", "ch": 12, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Lavoie measure bank liquidity by own funds in the pure credit economy?", "a": "With no government bonds or reserves, safe-assets ratios are always zero, so own funds are all that is left."}, {"id": "12.6", "ch": 12, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Give three of Lavoie’s four objections to the claim that capital ratios constrain lending in general.", "a": "Any three: ratios bind only for the riskiest banks; retained earnings and share issues; accounting flexibility in risk weights; banks’ reluctance reflects return on equity."}, {"id": "12.7", "ch": 12, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does he say a new loan raises B/OF only temporarily?", "a": "Higher loans bring higher net interest income, retained earnings add to own funds and bring the ratio back."}, {"id": "12.8", "ch": 12, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why can both a rise in the benchmark rate and a fall in bankers’ confidence widen the rationing gap?", "a": "A higher rate reduces notional demand and makes more borrowers fail the criteria (or adverse selection); lower confidence raises the risk premium and shifts the effective curve left."}, {"id": "12.9", "ch": 12, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A bank has own funds Rs 50, loans Rs 500. A new loan of Rs 50 is made. Compute B/OF before and after. If annual retained earnings are Rs 5 and no new lending occurs, what is B/OF a year later?", "a": "Before: 500/50 = 10. After: 550/50 = 11. A year later: 550/55 = 10."}, {"id": "12.10", "ch": 12, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Draw (or describe) Figure 12.1 for a case where a regulator announces climate-related tightening: where does each picture predict the change?", "a": "S–W: the hump moves down (or r∗ shifts left) if safe borrowers are more affected; Lavoie–Wolfson: effective demand shifts left for exposed borrowers, widening the gap and raising σB ."}, {"id": "12.11", "ch": 12, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Your data show that spreads on loans to coal-linked firms rose while volumes fell. Which interpretation (S–W or Lavoie–Wolfson) does this favour? What additional evidence would separate them?", "a": "Rising spreads with falling volumes are consistent with both; they favour neither alone. To separate: S–W predicts that raising the rate worsens the pool (higher default among those who remain), while L–W predicts tighter non-price standards (collateral, covenants) and rationing that follows confidence shocks rather than rate changes."}, {"id": "12.12", "ch": 12, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“If banks create credit ex nihilo, capital adequacy ratios are pointless.” Respond.", "a": "Not pointless: Lavoie says capital matters for risk and binds for risky banks and in systemic crises; the question is which constraint is binding, not whether credit is created by lending."}, {"id": "12.13", "ch": 12, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Lavoie says rationing comes from confidence, Stiglitz–Weiss from information. Which is right?” Answer with reference to what each can explain and what each can be tested on.", "a": "They are not mutually exclusive. S–W gives a micro mechanism that holds even with stable confidence; Lavoie emphasises that freezes in crises come with rising uncertainty and the shift in bankers’ expectations. Test: whether rationing varies with the information environment (e.g. disclosure mandates) or with confidence indicators."}, {"id": "13.1", "ch": 13, "lv": "Recall", "q": "In Stiglitz–Weiss, state the definition of credit rationing and why the bank’s expected return is not monotonic in the interest rate.", "a": "Rationing: some borrowers get no loan even though they would pay the quoted rate, or some obtain less than they wish, and no rise in the rate clears the market. A higher rate drives out safer borrowers (adverse selection) and induces riskier projects (incentive), so the pool worsens and the bank’s return can fall."}, {"id": "13.2", "ch": 13, "lv": "Recall", "q": "In Tirole’s fixed-investment model, what is pledgeable income, and what is the incentivecompatibility condition?", "a": "Pledgeable income: the part of project returns that can be promised to outsiders without destroying the borrower’s incentive, pH (R − B/∆p). IC: (∆p)Rb ≥ B."}, {"id": "13.3", "ch": 13, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Define the three forms of market efficiency and give one test for each.", "a": "Weak: past prices; test serial correlation or filter rules. Semi-strong: all public information; event study. Strong: all information, including private; mutual-fund performance and insider trading studies."}, {"id": "13.4", "ch": 13, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does Lavoie say is the cause of credit rationing? What is the mainstream answer?", "a": "Lavoie: loss of confidence and changes in bankers’ liquidity preference under uncertainty (with asymmetric information allowed as a minor cause). Mainstream: asymmetric information."}, {"id": "13.5", "ch": 13, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is collateral not a free fix for adverse selection in Stiglitz–Weiss (Theorems 9–12)?", "a": "Theorem 12: raising the collateral requirement drives out less wealthy, more cautious borrowers, so the average borrower who remains is riskier; this selection effect can outweigh the direct gain to the bank from collateral (Theorem 11). Theorem 10 adds that, with decreasing absolute risk aversion, wealthier borrowers undertake riskier projects, so collateral screens by wealth, not by safety."}, {"id": "13.6", "ch": 13, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Tirole’s equity multiplier k = 1/(1 − ρ0 ) rise with pledgeable income per unit of investment ρ0 ? What is the connection with capital adequacy ratios?", "a": "ρ0 is what can be pledged to outsiders per unit invested. The larger it is (a lower agency cost B), the more of each unit outsiders will finance, so each unit of own funds supports k = 1/(1−ρ0 ) units of investment. Capital adequacy is the bank-level analogue: capital divided by assets caps the lending multiple at its inverse."}, {"id": "13.7", "ch": 13, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Stiglitz say rigid capital standards may destabilise? Use the multiplier arithmetic.", "a": "With 8% capital, each rupee of capital supports Rs 12.5 of assets; a loss of Rs 10 requires a cut of Rs 125 unless capital is raised, which reduces asset prices and capital again."}, {"id": "13.8", "ch": 13, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is the joint-hypothesis problem a problem for CRISK-style measures?", "a": "CRISK uses equity returns and so assumes prices carry information about beliefs; if prices do not reflect Knightian-uncertain climate risk, the measure is conditional on market beliefs. Any test of its content is joint with a model of normal returns."}, {"id": "13.9", "ch": 13, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Using the S–W example (safe p = .9, R = 1.9; risky p = .5, R = 2.4), compute the rate at which the borrower is indifferent between the projects, and the bank’s return per rupee at r = 20% and r = 30%.", "a": "1 + r∗ = (1.71 − 1.20)/0.4 = 1.275, so r∗ = 27.5%. Bank return per rupee: 1.08 at 20%; 0.65 at 30%."}, {"id": "13.10", "ch": 13, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A firm has I = 100, R = 150, pH = .8, pL = .5, B = 15. Compute NPV, minimum net worth Ā and agency rent.", "a": "NPV = pH R−I = .8×150−100 = 20. Ā = pH B/∆p−(pH R−I) = .8×15/.3−20 = 20. Agency rent = pH B/∆p = 40."}, {"id": "13.11", "ch": 13, "lv": "Apply", "q": "You regress bank lending on capital ratio and find a significant effect only when ratios are within 1 percentage point of the minimum. Which authors does that support, and why?", "a": "It supports the view that the constraint matters mainly for banks near the minimum (Lavoie’s first objection) and in stress (Stiglitz). Under Tirole, it would show up as a multiplier limit for low-net-worth lenders."}, {"id": "13.12", "ch": 13, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Credit rationing is a theoretical curiosity; real banks just charge a higher rate for riskier loans.” Answer using Stiglitz–Weiss, Lavoie’s Kalecki figure and the credit-line argument.", "a": "Stiglitz–Weiss: a higher rate may lower the bank’s expected return, so the bank prefers rationing to repricing. Lavoie’s Figure 4.5 (Kalecki): the rate does rise with leverage across firms, so price differentiation exists, but it coexists with limits on credit lines, which are contracts specifying maximum amounts. Rationing is about quantity limits at the offered price."}, {"id": "13.13", "ch": 13, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Your thesis uses Fama’s event study and Lavoie’s non-ergodicity. Isn’t that incoherent?” Respond in six sentences.", "a": "Not incoherent if the levels are stated: Fama’s method is a measurement tool that does not require believing efficiency; the event study can be read as testing whether information is incorporated under a specified model; non-ergodicity is motivation for scenarios. Be explicit that results are conditional on the model of normal returns and on the sample period. A joint test is exactly what Fama says every test is."}, {"id": "13.14", "ch": 13, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Is the Indian public-sector bank an example of Stiglitz’s model, Lavoie’s, or neither?” Give a position with two pieces of evidence you would collect.", "a": "A defensible answer: partly Stiglitz (capital constraints and state recapitalisation) and partly Lavoie (government as capital provider; weak market discipline); neither fully, because public-sector banks face directed lending and implicit guarantees. Evidence: (i) lending growth against capital ratio near the minimum; (ii) spread and non-price standards by sector before and after a policy or disclosure change; (iii) recapitalisation episodes."}]};